const char FACT_TEXT[] =
    "metallic hydrogen\x00"""
    "Deep inside Jupiter,\nhydrogen is squeezed\ninto a liquid metal.\n\nHydrogen is the\nsimplest element,\nand on Earth it is a\ngas that doesn't\nconduct electricity.\nBut in 1935,\nphysicists predicted\nthat at enormous\npressure its atoms\nwould be squeezed so\nclose that their\nelectrons would be\nshared freely,\nturning it into a\nmetal.\n\nInside Jupiter,\npressures reach\nmillions of times\nair pressure, and\nmuch of the planet's\nhydrogen is thought\nto be in this state:\na vast ocean of\nliquid metal, whose\nchurning makes\nJupiter's huge\nmagnetic field, the\nstrongest of any\nplanet.\n\nMaking it in a lab\nis extremely hard.\nExperiments\nsqueezing hydrogen\nbetween diamonds or\nwith shock waves\nhave reported signs\nof it, but some of\nthe claims are still\ndisputed.\x00"""
    "zero-point energy\x00"""
    "Nothing can ever be\nperfectly still.\nEven at absolute\nzero, atoms still\njiggle.\n\nCool something down\nand its atoms move\nless and less. You\nmight expect that at\nabsolute zero, minus\n273.15 degrees\nCelsius, they would\nstop completely.\n\nQuantum physics says\nthey can't. An atom\nsitting perfectly\nstill in a perfectly\nknown place would\nbreak the\nuncertainty\nprinciple. So even\nat its lowest\npossible energy it\nkeeps a restless\nmotion, called\nzero-point energy.\n\nHelium shows it\nbest. Its atoms are\nso light and attract\neach other so weakly\nthat this jiggling\nalone keeps it\nliquid. At normal\npressure, helium\nnever freezes,\nhowever cold you\nmake it. You have to\nsqueeze it to about\n25 times air\npressure.\x00"""
    "electron waves\x00"""
    "Electron microscopes\nsee atoms because\nelectrons are waves\ntoo, far shorter\nthan light.\n\nA microscope can't\nshow details smaller\nthan about half the\nwavelength of the\nlight it uses.\nVisible light waves\nare around 500\nnanometres long, and\natoms are thousands\nof times smaller, so\nlight can never show\nthem.\nIn 1924, Louis de\nBroglie proposed\nthat every particle\nis also a wave, and\nthe faster it moves,\nthe shorter its\nwavelength.\nElectrons sped up by\na high voltage have\nwavelengths far\nsmaller than an\natom.\nSo electron\nmicroscopes focus\nbeams of electrons,\nwith magnets as\nlenses. The best can\nsee individual\natoms, and with a\nversion called\ncryo-electron\nmicroscopy,\nbiologists see the\nshapes of single\nproteins, which won\nthe 2017 Nobel Prize\nin Chemistry.\x00"""
    "pulsars\x00"""
    "Some dead stars tick\nas steadily as\natomic clocks.\n\nA pulsar is a\nspinning neutron\nstar that sweeps\nbeams of radio waves\nacross space, like a\nlighthouse. Jocelyn\nBell Burnell\ndiscovered the first\none in 1967, as a\nstudent. Its pulses\nwere so regular that\nthe team\nhalf-jokingly\nlabelled it LGM-1,\nfor little green\nmen.\n\nThe fastest,\nmillisecond pulsars,\nspin hundreds of\ntimes a second, and\nover years their\nticks are so steady\nthat they rival\natomic clocks.\n\nIn 2023, after\ntiming dozens of\nthem for 15 years,\nastronomers\nannounced tiny\nshared wobbles in\ntheir ticks:\nevidence of a\nbackground of slow\ngravitational waves\nrolling through our\ngalaxy, probably\nfrom pairs of giant\nblack holes orbiting\neach other across\nthe universe.\x00"""
    "the best prediction\x00"""
    "One theory predicts\nhow magnetic the\nelectron is,\nmatching experiment\nto 12 digits.\n\nQuantum\nelectrodynamics, or\nQED, is the quantum\ntheory of light and\nelectrons. Among\nmany other things,\nit predicts how\nstrong a magnet the\nelectron is, because\nof its spin.\n\nThe simplest\ncalculation gives\nexactly 2, in\nnatural units. But\nQED says the\nelectron is\nsurrounded by a fizz\nof short-lived\nparticles appearing\nand vanishing in the\nempty space around\nit, and each adds a\ntiny correction.\nPhysicists have\ncalculated over ten\nthousand of them.\n\nThe result, about\n2.00231930436,\nmatches the\nmeasurement to about\none part in a\ntrillion. Richard\nFeynman, who helped\nbuild QED, compared\nit to measuring the\nwidth of America to\nwithin a hair, and\ncalled QED the jewel\nof physics.\x00"""
    "rainbows\x00"""
    "No two people see\nthe same rainbow.\nEach sees light from\na different set of\nraindrops.\n\nA rainbow forms when\nsunlight enters\nraindrops, reflects\noff the back of them\nand comes out again,\nsplit into colors,\nbecause each color\nbends by a slightly\ndifferent amount.\n\nEach drop sends red\nlight out at about\n42 degrees from the\ndirection of the\nsunlight, and violet\nat about 40. So you\nsee red from all the\ndrops 42 degrees\naway from the point\ndirectly opposite\nthe Sun: a circle\naround the shadow of\nyour own head. The\nperson next to you\nsees a circle around\ntheirs, made by\nother drops.\n\nLight bouncing twice\ninside the drops\nmakes a fainter\nsecond bow outside\nthe first, with its\ncolors reversed.\nBetween the two, the\nsky is darker: a\nband named after\nAlexander of\nAphrodisias, who\ndescribed it 1,800\nyears ago.\x00"""
    "laser cooling\x00"""
    "Shine lasers on\natoms the right way\nand they cool to\nmillionths of a\ndegree.\n\nLight usually heats\nthings. But each\nphoton an atom\nabsorbs gives it a\nlittle kick. Tune a\nlaser to a color\njust slightly too\nred for an atom to\nabsorb while it sits\nstill.\n\nAn atom moving\ntoward the laser\nsees its light\nshifted toward blue\nby the Doppler\neffect, just enough\nto absorb it, and\ngets kicked\nbackwards, slowing\ndown. Atoms moving\naway don't absorb\nit. Put lasers on\nall six sides and\nevery atom is slowed\nwhichever way it\nmoves, as if wading\nthrough syrup:\noptical molasses.\n\nThis cools atoms to\nmillionths of a\ndegree above\nabsolute zero in a\nfraction of a\nsecond. It won the\n1997 Nobel Prize,\nand it is the first\nstep for atomic\nclocks,\nBose-Einstein\ncondensates and many\nquantum computers.\x00"""
    "quantum levitation\x00"""
    "A superconductor can\nlock itself in\nmidair above a\nmagnet, even upside\ndown.\n\nSuperconductors push\nmagnetic fields out\nof themselves. This\nis the Meissner\neffect, and it is\nenough to make a\nmagnet float above a\ncold superconductor.\n\nIn many\nsuperconductors,\nthough, thin threads\nof magnetic field\nare let through at\ntiny defects, each\nthread carrying\nexactly one quantum\nof magnetism. The\nmaterial then grips\nthe threads where\nthey are. This is\ncalled flux pinning.\n\nSo a superconductor\nover a magnet\ndoesn't just float:\nit stays at whatever\nheight and tilt you\nput it, as if stuck\nin invisible foam,\nand it can even hang\nunder the magnet.\nSet it on a circular\ntrack of magnets,\nand it glides around\nwithout ever falling\noff.\x00"""
    "a black hole photo\x00"""
    "In 2019, astronomers\nphotographed a black\nhole's shadow with\nan Earth-sized\ntelescope.\n\nA black hole gives\noff no light, but\nthe gas swirling\naround it does,\nglowing hot as it\nfalls in. Light\npassing near the\nhole is bent around\nit, so it looks like\na bright ring around\na dark shadow, about\ntwo and a half times\nwider than the\nhorizon.\n\nThe black hole in\nthe galaxy M87 is\n6.5 billion times\nthe mass of the Sun,\nbut it's 55 million\nlight-years away, so\nits ring looks as\nsmall as an orange\non the Moon would.\nSeeing it needs a\ntelescope as big as\nthe Earth.\n\nThe Event Horizon\nTelescope linked\nradio dishes from\nSpain to the South\nPole, recording at\nthe same instant,\ntimed by atomic\nclocks, and combined\ntheir data with\nsupercomputers. In\n2022 it pictured the\nblack hole at the\ncentre of our own\ngalaxy.\x00"""
    "haidinger's brush\x00"""
    "You can learn to see\nwhether light is\npolarized: look for\na faint yellow bow\ntie.\n\nLight is a wave of\nelectric and\nmagnetic fields, and\nthe direction in\nwhich its electric\nfield wiggles is its\npolarization. Light\nfrom an LCD screen,\nand from the blue\nsky away from the\nSun, is partly\npolarized. Bees and\nmany other animals\nnavigate by it.\n\nHumans seem blind to\nit, but not quite.\nLook at a plain\nwhite area on an LCD\nscreen and tilt your\nhead slowly from\nside to side. Many\npeople notice a\nfaint yellowish\nbow-tie shape at the\nvery centre of their\nvision, turning as\nthey tilt. This is\nHaidinger's brush,\ndescribed in 1844.\nIt comes from\npigment molecules at\nthe centre of the\nretina, arranged in\nrings, which absorb\nblue light polarized\none way more than\nthe other.\x00"""
    "bose-einstein gas\x00"""
    "Cool atoms close to\nabsolute zero, and\nthousands of them\nmerge into a single\nquantum wave.\n\nEvery atom is also a\nwave, and the colder\nit is, the longer\nthat wave. At room\ntemperature the wave\nis far smaller than\nthe atom. But a few\nbillionths of a\ndegree above\nabsolute zero, it\ngrows larger than\nthe gaps between the\natoms.\n\nThen, for atoms of\nthe right kind\n(bosons), something\nremarkable happens.\nThe waves overlap\nand lock together,\nand thousands or\nmillions of atoms\nfall into the very\nsame state, behaving\nas one giant atom\nyou can almost see.\n\nSatyendra Nath Bose\nand Einstein\npredicted it in\n1924. It took until\n1995 to cool a gas\nenough to make one,\nwhich won the 2001\nNobel Prize. The\natoms in it move so\nperfectly in step\nthat two of these\ncondensates\ninterfere like waves\nof light.\x00"""
    "supercooled water\x00"""
    "Pure water can stay\nliquid far below 0\ndegrees. It needs\nsomething to freeze\naround.\n\nWater doesn't freeze\njust because it is\ncold enough. Ice has\nto start somewhere:\na tiny seed crystal,\nwhich usually forms\non dust, a scratch\nin the container or\na bubble.\n\nVery pure, still\nwater has none, so\nit can stay liquid\nwell below freezing:\nin labs, down to\nabout minus 40\ndegrees, and tiny\ndroplets even\ncolder. This is\nsupercooled water.\nTap the bottle or\ndrop in a crystal of\nice, and ice shoots\nthrough it in\nseconds.\n\nClouds are full of\nsupercooled\ndroplets. When they\nhit an aircraft's\nwing, they freeze on\ncontact, which is\nwhy planes need\nde-icing systems.\x00"""
    "the dark night\x00"""
    "Why is the night sky\ndark? The answer\nshows that the\nuniverse had a\nbeginning.\n\nIf the universe were\ninfinite, unchanging\nand filled with\nstars forever, every\nline of sight would\nend on the surface\nof a star. The whole\nnight sky would\nblaze as brightly as\nthe Sun. This puzzle\nis called Olbers'\nparadox.\nThe dark sky tells\nus something is\nwrong with that\npicture. The main\nanswer is that the\nuniverse has a\nfinite age: light\nfrom stars too far\naway hasn't had time\nto reach us, and\nstars haven't been\nshining forever.\nThe expansion helps\ntoo, stretching\ndistant light into\ndimmer, redder\nwaves. In a way, the\nsky does glow in\nevery direction: the\ncosmic microwave\nbackground, the Big\nBang's light\nstretched far beyond\nwhat eyes can see.\nThe writer Edgar\nAllan Poe guessed\nthe right answer in\n1848.\x00"""
    "the hoyle state\x00"""
    "The carbon in you\nexists thanks to an\nenergy level\npredicted because\nlife exists.\n\nStars make carbon by\nfusing three helium\nnuclei. But two\nhelium nuclei stuck\ntogether,\nberyllium-8, fall\napart in less than a\nmillionth of a\nbillionth of a\nsecond, so a third\nmust hit in that\ninstant. At first\nsight, that should\nmake almost no\ncarbon.\n\nIn 1953, Fred Hoyle\nargued that since\ncarbon is plentiful\n(we are made of it),\nthe carbon nucleus\nmust have an energy\nlevel matching the\nenergy of the three\nheliums: a\nresonance, which\nmakes the reaction\nfar more likely.\nHoyle predicted its\nenergy.\nExperimenters\nlooked, and found it\nwhere Hoyle said. It\nis now called the\nHoyle state, and it\nis often given as an\nexample of how\nfinely tuned the\nlaws of nature seem\nto be for life.\x00"""
    "the number 137\x00"""
    "A pure number, about\n1/137, sets how\nstrongly light and\nmatter interact.\n\nMost numbers in\nphysics depend on\nunits like metres\nand seconds. The\nfine-structure\nconstant doesn't. It\ncombines the\nelectron's charge,\nthe speed of light\nand Planck's\nconstant into a pure\nnumber, about 1/137,\nthe same for aliens\nusing any units at\nall.\n\nIt sets how strongly\nelectrons and light\npull on each other,\nand so the size of\natoms, the colors\nthey give off, and\nall of chemistry. If\nit were a few\npercent different,\nstars might not be\nable to make carbon,\nand there might be\nno us.\n\nNobody knows why it\nhas this value.\nWolfgang Pauli was\nfascinated by 137,\nand the story goes\nthat Pauli died in\nhospital room 137.\nNo theory has ever\nderived the number\nfrom anything\ndeeper.\x00"""
    "flying clocks\x00"""
    "In 1971, atomic\nclocks flown around\nthe world came back\nout of step with\nthose at home.\n\nRelativity says\nmoving clocks run\nslow, and clocks\nhigher up, in weaker\ngravity, run fast.\nIn 1971, Joseph\nHafele and Richard\nKeating tested both\nby taking atomic\nclocks on ordinary\npassenger flights\naround the world,\nonce eastward and\nonce westward.\n\nWhen they landed and\nwere compared with\nclocks that had\nstayed at the US\nNaval Observatory,\nthe eastbound clocks\nhad lost about 60\nbillionths of a\nsecond, and the\nwestbound ones had\ngained about 270, as\nrelativity\npredicted. The\ndirections differ\nbecause Earth spins:\nflying east adds the\nplane's speed to the\nground's.\n\nThat is the twin\nparadox made real. A\ntwin who travels to\na star and back at\nnearly light speed\nreally does come\nhome younger than\nthe one who stayed,\nbecause the two took\ndifferent paths\nthrough spacetime.\x00"""
    "dark energy\x00"""
    "The expansion of the\nuniverse is speeding\nup, pushed by what\nwe call dark energy.\n\nAfter the Big Bang,\ngravity should have\nbeen slowing the\nexpansion of the\nuniverse. In 1998,\ntwo teams measured\nby how much, using\nexploding stars\nwhose brightness\ntells their\ndistance.\n\nInstead, distant\nsupernovae were\nfainter than\nexpected: the\nexpansion has been\nspeeding up for the\nlast few billion\nyears. The discovery\nwon the 2011 Nobel\nPrize.\n\nWhatever drives it\nis called dark\nenergy, and it makes\nup about 70 percent\nof the universe's\nenergy. It could be\na property of empty\nspace, but quantum\ntheory's estimate\nfor that is off by a\nfactor of about\n10^120, the worst\nprediction in\nphysics. Recent\nsurveys hint that\ndark energy may be\nweakening over time.\x00"""
    "dark matter\x00"""
    "Galaxies spin so\nfast they should fly\napart. Something\ninvisible holds them\ntogether.\n\nIn the solar system,\nthe far planets\norbit slowly,\nbecause most of the\nmass is in the\ncentre. Galaxies\nwere expected to\nwork the same way,\nwith their outer\nstars slower. In the\n1970s, Vera Rubin\nmeasured them, and\nfound the outer\nstars move about as\nfast as the inner\nones.\n\nSo there must be far\nmore mass than we\nsee, spread in a\nhalo around each\ngalaxy. This dark\nmatter gives off and\nabsorbs no light,\nbut its gravity\nshows up everywhere:\nin clusters of\ngalaxies, in\ngravitational\nlensing, in the\npattern of the\ncosmic microwave\nbackground.\n\nThere is about five\ntimes as much dark\nmatter as ordinary\nmatter. Nobody knows\nwhat it is. Huge\ndetectors deep\nunderground wait for\na dark matter\nparticle to bump\ninto an atom. None\nhas, so far.\x00"""
    "e = mc^2\x00"""
    "A hot cup of coffee\nweighs slightly more\nthan a cold one.\nEnergy has mass.\n\nEinstein's E = mc^2\nsays mass and energy\nare the same thing,\nexchanged at the\nrate of the speed of\nlight squared. That\nnumber is huge, so a\nlittle mass is a lot\nof energy, and a lot\nof energy is only a\nlittle mass.\n\nAnything that gains\nenergy gains mass. A\ncup of coffee heated\nby 60 degrees gains\nalmost a billionth\nof a gram. A charged\nphone battery is\nheavier than a flat\none, by less than a\nbillionth of a gram.\nFar too little to\nweigh, but real.\n\nIn nuclear reactions\nit is big enough to\nmeasure: the\nproducts of fusion\nin the Sun weigh\nabout 0.7 percent\nless than what went\nin. That missing\nmass is the energy\nof sunlight. The Sun\nloses about 4\nmillion tons of mass\nevery second this\nway.\x00"""
    "liquid crystals\x00"""
    "Many screens use\nliquid crystals: a\nstate of matter\nbetween liquid and\nsolid.\n\nIn a liquid,\nmolecules tumble\nrandomly. In a\ncrystal, they sit in\na fixed grid. Some\nrod-shaped molecules\ndo something in\nbetween: they flow\nlike a liquid, but\nall point in roughly\nthe same direction,\nlike logs floating\ndown a river.\n\nBecause they are\naligned, liquid\ncrystals twist\npolarized light, and\nan electric field\ncan turn them, which\nchanges the twist.\nPut them between two\npolarizing filters,\nand each tiny region\ncan let light\nthrough or block it.\n\nThat is how an LCD\nworks: millions of\npixels, each a\nlittle shutter of\nliquid crystal. The\nstate was discovered\nin 1888 in a\nsubstance from\ncarrots, which\nseemed to have two\nmelting points.\x00"""
    "the speed of light\x00"""
    "The speed of light\nisn't really about\nlight. It is the top\nspeed of cause and\neffect.\n\nRelativity grew from\nthe discovery that\nlight moves at the\nsame speed for\neveryone, however\nfast they are going.\nBut this speed, c,\nis deeper than\nlight: it is built\ninto the geometry of\nspace and time, as\nthe speed limit of\nany signal of any\nkind.\n\nAnything without\nmass must move at\nexactly c: light,\nand gravitational\nwaves too. In 2017,\ngravitational waves\nand light from\ncolliding neutron\nstars 130 million\nlight-years away\narrived within two\nseconds of each\nother, which shows\nthat gravity travels\nat the speed of\nlight, to better\nthan a part in a\nthousand trillion.\n\nIf the Sun suddenly\nvanished, Earth\nwould keep circling\nits empty spot for\neight minutes, until\nthe news arrived.\x00"""
    "the double slit\x00"""
    "Fire electrons one\nat a time at two\nslits, and each one\ninterferes with\nitself.\n\nSend water waves\nthrough two gaps and\nthey spread out and\noverlap. Where crest\nmeets crest they add\nup, where crest\nmeets trough they\ncancel, and you get\na striped pattern.\nThat is\ninterference, the\nsignature of waves.\nNow fire electrons\nat a screen through\ntwo narrow slits, so\nslowly that only one\nis in flight at a\ntime. Each arrives\nas a single dot,\nlike a particle. But\nafter thousands of\ndots, the stripes of\ninterference appear.\n\nEach electron,\nalone, travelled as\na wave that went\nthrough both slits\nand interfered with\nitself, then landed\nin one spot. Put a\ndetector at the\nslits to see which\none it takes, and\nthe stripes vanish.\nRichard Feynman said\nthis experiment\nholds the only real\nmystery of quantum\nmechanics.\x00"""
    "earth's young core\x00"""
    "Because of\nrelativity, Earth's\ncentre is 2.5 years\nyounger than its\nsurface.\n\nGeneral relativity\nsays time runs\nslower deeper in a\ngravity well. That\nisn't the same as\nwhere gravity pulls\nhardest: at the\ncentre of the Earth\nthere is no pull at\nall, but it is the\ndeepest point of the\nwell.\nOver the 4.5 billion\nyears of Earth's\nhistory, the small\ndifference adds up.\nIn 2016, physicists\ncalculated that the\ncore is about two\nand a half years\nyounger than the\ncrust.\n\nRichard Feynman once\nestimated the\ndifference as a day\nor two, a rare slip.\nThe calculation is\nsimple, but it is\neasy to get wrong.\x00"""
    "flipping poles\x00"""
    "Earth's magnetic\nnorth and south\npoles have swapped\nplaces hundreds of\ntimes.\n\nEarth's magnetic\nfield is made by\nmolten iron swirling\nin its outer core,\nabout 3,000\nkilometres below us.\nMoving iron carries\nelectric currents,\nwhich make magnetic\nfields, which steer\nthe flowing iron: a\nself-sustaining\ndynamo.\n\nThe dynamo is\nchaotic, and every\nso often the field\nweakens and flips,\nso a compass would\npoint south. The\nrecord is written in\nthe rock of the\nocean floor: as lava\ncools, its iron\nminerals freeze in\nthe direction of the\nfield at the time,\nleaving stripes of\nalternating\nmagnetism.\nThe last full flip\nwas about 780,000\nyears ago. Flips\ncome irregularly,\nfrom tens of\nthousands to tens of\nmillions of years\napart, and take\nthousands of years.\nThe field is\nweakening now, but\nnobody can say\nwhether a flip is\ncoming.\x00"""
    "lagrange points\x00"""
    "There are five\nplaces near Earth\nwhere a spacecraft\ncan park, held by\ntwo gravities.\n\nUsually, the farther\na spacecraft is from\nthe Sun, the slower\nit orbits. But\nEarth's gravity adds\nto the Sun's. At a\npoint 1.5 million\nkilometres beyond\nEarth, the extra\npull is exactly\nenough for a craft\nto circle the Sun in\none year, keeping\npace with Earth.\n\nThat point, L2, is\none of five Lagrange\npoints, where\ngravity and orbital\nmotion balance,\nworked out by\nLeonhard Euler and\nJoseph-Louis\nLagrange in the\n1700s. The James\nWebb Space Telescope\norbits around L2,\nkeeping the Sun,\nEarth and Moon on\none side, behind its\nsunshield.\n\nTwo of the points,\nL4 and L5, are\nstable, so natural\nobjects collect\nthere. Jupiter has\nthousands of\nasteroids, called\nTrojans, sharing its\norbit at its L4 and\nL5.\x00"""
    "two tides\x00"""
    "Why are there two\nhigh tides a day,\nnot one? Because\ngravity stretches.\n\nThe Moon pulls on\nthe whole Earth, but\ngravity weakens with\ndistance, so it\npulls the near side\na bit more strongly\nthan the centre, and\nthe centre a bit\nmore than the far\nside.\n\nSo the Earth is\ngently stretched\nalong the line to\nthe Moon. The near\nocean is pulled\ntoward the Moon a\nbit more than the\nEarth is, and the\nEarth a bit more\nthan the far ocean,\nwhich is left\nbehind. There's a\nbulge on both sides,\nand as Earth turns,\neach place passes\nthrough both bulges\nevery day.\n\nThat stretching is a\ntidal force. Near a\nblack hole, the same\neffect, made\nenormous, stretches\nanything that falls\nin. Tides from\nJupiter knead its\nmoon Io so hard that\nit is the most\nvolcanic place in\nthe solar system.\x00"""
    "phonons\x00"""
    "Sound in a crystal\ncomes in little\npackets, the way\nlight comes in\nphotons.\n\nThe atoms of a\ncrystal are linked\nlike balls on\nsprings, so\nvibrations ripple\nthrough it as waves.\nHeat in a solid is\nmostly this\njiggling, and sound\nis a slower, more\norderly version of\nit.\nQuantum physics says\nthose vibrations\ncan't have any\nenergy they like:\nthey come in\npackets, called\nphonons. Physicists\ntreat phonons as\nparticles that are\nborn, travel, bounce\noff each other and\ndisappear.\nPhonons explain why\ndiamond carries heat\nso well and glass so\npoorly. They help\nelectrons pair up in\nsuperconductors. And\ntoday, single\nphonons can be made\nand detected in tiny\ndevices, and used to\ncarry quantum\ninformation.\x00"""
    "neutron stars\x00"""
    "A teaspoon of\nneutron star would\nweigh about a\nbillion tons on\nEarth.\n\nWhen a star much\nheavier than the Sun\nruns out of fuel,\nits core collapses\nin under a second.\nElectrons are\npressed into\nprotons, making\nneutrons, and the\ncore shrinks to a\nball about 20\nkilometres across\nwith more mass than\nthe Sun.\n\nIt is held up only\nby the neutrons\nrefusing to be\nsqueezed into the\nsame states: the\nPauli exclusion\nprinciple again. It\nis as dense as an\natomic nucleus. A\nsugar cube of it\nwould weigh about as\nmuch as all the\npeople on Earth\ntogether.\n\nShrinking makes it\nspin faster, like a\nskater pulling in\ntheir arms: the\nfastest known\nneutron star turns\n716 times a second.\nIts gravity is so\nstrong that its\ntallest mountains\nare thought to be\nless than a\nmillimetre high.\x00"""
    "the round electron\x00"""
    "The electron is the\nroundest thing\nanyone has ever\nmeasured.\n\nIf the electron's\ncharge were slightly\nlopsided, with a bit\nmore at one end than\nthe other, it would\ntwist in an electric\nfield. Many theories\nthat go beyond\ntoday's physics\npredict such a\nlopsidedness, called\nan electric dipole\nmoment.\nExperiments look for\nit by watching\nelectrons inside\nmolecules, where the\nelectric fields are\nenormous, and\nchecking whether\ntheir spins wobble.\nNone has been seen.\nIf an electron were\nblown up to the size\nof the solar system,\nany lopsidedness\nwould have to be\nthinner than a human\nhair.\n\nEach non-result\nrules out ideas,\nsuch as some\nversions of\nsupersymmetry, that\nwould have made the\nelectron less round.\nA tabletop\nexperiment, testing\nphysics at energies\nbeyond the biggest\nparticle collider.\x00"""
    "proton decay\x00"""
    "Protons may not last\nforever. We have\nwatched for decades\nand seen none decay.\n\nMany theories that\nunify the forces of\nnature predict that\nprotons, the heart\nof every atom,\nshould very rarely\ndecay into lighter\nparticles.\n\nTo test it,\nSuper-Kamiokande in\nJapan watches 50,000\ntons of ultra-pure\nwater deep in a\nmine: about 10^34\nprotons. If protons\nlasted 10^34 years\non average, a few\nwould decay every\nyear. None has been\nseen.\n\nSo protons, if they\ndecay at all, last\nover 10^34 years, a\ntrillion trillion\ntimes the age of the\nuniverse. That ruled\nout the simplest\nunified theories.\nBigger detectors,\nlike\nHyper-Kamiokande,\nare being built to\nkeep looking.\x00"""
    "the zeno effect\x00"""
    "Watch an unstable\nquantum system often\nenough, and you can\nstop it from\nchanging.\n\nA quantum system\nthat is changing,\nsay an atom about to\njump from one state\nto another, does it\nsmoothly at first:\nits chance of having\njumped grows very\nslowly at the start.\n\nEach measurement\nresets it: you find\nthat it has jumped\nor not, and if not,\nit starts again from\nthe slow beginning.\nMeasure it again and\nagain, quickly, and\nit hardly ever gets\nthe chance to jump.\n\nIt is named after\nZeno's paradox of\nthe arrow that can\nnever move. It was\nseen in 1990 with\ntrapped ions, whose\nchange between two\nstates was slowed by\nflashing them with\nlaser pulses. In the\nquantum world, a\nwatched pot really\nnever boils.\x00"""
    "superconductors\x00"""
    "In a superconductor,\na current meets zero\nresistance and can\ncircle for years.\n\nIn an ordinary wire,\nelectrons bump into\nthe vibrating atoms\nof the metal and\nlose energy as heat.\nThat is resistance.\nCool some materials\nenough, and it\nvanishes: not just\nsmall, but zero.\n\nThe trick is that\nthe electrons pair\nup. Each one pulls\nslightly on the\natoms around it, and\nthat dent attracts\nanother electron.\nThese Cooper pairs\nact like bosons and\nall fall into one\nshared quantum\nstate, which moves\nas a whole and can't\nbe slowed down a\nlittle at a time.\n\nCurrents started in\nsuperconducting\nrings have kept\nflowing for years\nwith no measurable\nloss.\nSuperconducting\ncoils make the\nstrong magnets in\nMRI scanners and in\nparticle\naccelerators like\nthe LHC.\x00"""
    "muons\x00"""
    "Particles made high\nin the sky reach the\nground only because\ntheir time runs\nslow.\n\nWhen cosmic rays hit\nthe top of the\natmosphere, they\nmake particles\ncalled muons, which\ndecay in about two\nmillionths of a\nsecond. Even at\nnearly the speed of\nlight, in that time\na muon should cover\nonly about 600\nmetres.\n\nYet huge numbers of\nthem reach the\nground, 15\nkilometres below.\nRoughly one passes\nthrough your hand\nevery second.\n\nThe reason is time\ndilation. To us, a\nmuon's clock runs\nslow because it\nmoves so fast, and\nits two microseconds\nstretch many times\nlonger. From the\nmuon's point of\nview, it's the\natmosphere that is\nsquashed thinner.\nEither way, it\narrives. Counting\nmuons at different\nheights was one of\nthe first tests of\nrelativity.\x00"""
    "entanglement\x00"""
    "Entangled particles\nstay linked: measure\none and you\ninstantly know the\nother, however far.\n\nTwo particles can be\nmade so that they\ndon't each have a\nstate of their own,\nonly a shared one.\nFor example, a pair\nwhose spins must\npoint opposite ways,\nthough neither has\npicked a direction\nyet.\n\nMeasure one and\nfind, say, up. At\nthat instant you\nknow the other is\ndown, even if it is\non the far side of\nthe galaxy. Einstein\ncalled this 'spooky\naction at a\ndistance' and\nsuspected that the\nparticles had\nsecretly decided all\nalong.\n\nExperiments say they\nhadn't (see Bell's\ntheorem). But you\ncan't use\nentanglement to send\na message: each\nresult on its own\nlooks perfectly\nrandom, and the\npattern only shows\nwhen the two sides\ncompare notes, by\nordinary,\nslower-than-light\nmeans.\x00"""
    "below absolute zero\x00"""
    "Some systems can\nhave negative\ntemperatures, and\nthey are hotter than\ninfinitely hot.\n\nTemperature isn't\nreally how fast\nthings move. It\ntells you how a\nsystem's disorder\nchanges as you add\nenergy. For most\nthings, adding\nenergy opens up more\nways to arrange\nthem, and the\ntemperature is\npositive.\n\nBut some systems\nhave a top energy,\nlike tiny magnets\nthat can only point\nup or down. Fill\nmost of them up to\nthe top state, and\nadding more energy\nmakes them more\norderly, not less.\nThen the formula\ngives a temperature\nbelow zero.\n\nSuch a system isn't\ncold. Put it in\ntouch with anything\nat a positive\ntemperature and heat\nflows out of it: it\nis hotter than any\npositive\ntemperature, even an\ninfinite one. It was\ndone with the spins\nof atomic nuclei in\n1951, and with a gas\nof moving atoms in\n2013.\x00"""
    "ball lightning\x00"""
    "Ball lightning has\nbeen seen for\ncenturies and\nfilmed, but nobody\ncan explain it.\n\nWitnesses describe\nglowing balls, from\nthe size of a pea to\na beach ball,\nfloating near the\nground during\nthunderstorms for\nseveral seconds,\nsometimes drifting\nthrough windows,\nbefore vanishing\nquietly or with a\nbang.\n\nFor a long time many\nscientists doubted\nit existed. In 2012,\nresearchers in\nChina, recording an\nordinary storm,\nhappened to film one\nand measure its\nlight. It contained\nelements from soil,\nsuch as silicon,\niron and calcium.\n\nThat supports one\nidea: lightning\nstriking the ground\nvaporizes soil, and\nthe hot vapor of\nsilicon burns slowly\nin the air as a\nglowing ball. But it\ncan't explain balls\nseen inside\naircraft. There are\ndozens of other\ntheories, and labs\ncan make glowing\nballs, but none\nquite like the real\nthing.\x00"""
    "fast radio bursts\x00"""
    "Radio flashes from\nother galaxies,\nlasting a thousandth\nof a second, are a\nmystery.\n\nIn 2007, astronomers\ndigging through old\nradio telescope data\nfound a single burst\nof radio waves,\nlasting a few\nthousandths of a\nsecond, from far\nbeyond our galaxy.\nIn that blink, such\nbursts give off as\nmuch energy as the\nSun does in days, or\neven years.\n\nThousands have been\nfound since. Some\nrepeat, most flash\nonly once. In 2020,\none was caught\ncoming from a\nmagnetar inside our\nown galaxy, so at\nleast some come from\nthese super-magnetic\nneutron stars.\n\nEach burst is also a\nprobe. On the way to\nus, the longer radio\nwaves lag behind the\nshorter ones, by an\namount that counts\nthe electrons along\nthe path. That has\nbeen used to find\nordinary matter\nhiding in the thin\ngas between\ngalaxies.\x00"""
    "mri\x00"""
    "An MRI scanner makes\npictures by tipping\nover the tiny\nmagnets in your\nhydrogen atoms.\n\nThe nucleus of a\nhydrogen atom, a\nsingle proton, has\nspin, which makes it\na tiny magnet. Your\nbody is mostly\nwater, so it's\npacked with them.\n\nIn the strong field\nof an MRI scanner,\nthe protons line up\nslightly and wobble\naround the field\nlike spinning tops,\nat a rate set by the\nfield's strength. A\npulse of radio waves\nat exactly that rate\ntips them over. When\nit stops, they swing\nback and send out a\nfaint radio signal.\n\nHow fast they settle\ndepends on the\ntissue they're in,\nwhich gives the\ncontrast between,\nsay, fat and muscle.\nVarying the field\nacross the body\nmakes the protons in\neach spot wobble at\na slightly different\nrate, so the signal\ncan be mapped in 3D.\nNo X-rays, only\nspins.\x00"""
    "time crystals\x00"""
    "A time crystal\nrepeats in time the\nway a crystal\nrepeats in space,\nwithout heating up.\n\nA crystal is matter\nthat repeats in\nspace: its atoms sit\nin a pattern. In\n2012, Frank Wilczek\nasked whether matter\ncould also repeat in\ntime, cycling\nforever in its\nlowest energy state.\nIt was soon proved\nthat it can't, at\nleast not in that\nform.\n\nBut a variant can.\nKick a group of\nquantum spins with a\nregular pulse, and\nthey flip back and\nforth in a rhythm of\ntheir own, for\nexample at twice the\nperiod of the kicks.\nIt's like a jelly\nyou poke once a\nsecond that wobbles\nonce every two\nseconds, and keeps\nthat rhythm even if\nthe pokes are a\nlittle off.\n\nNormally, kicking a\nsystem like this\nwould heat it up\ninto chaos. A time\ncrystal doesn't heat\nup. One was made in\n2021 on Google's\nquantum computer,\nwith 20 qubits.\x00"""
    "pauli exclusion\x00"""
    "No two electrons can\nshare the same\nstate. That's why\nyou don't sink\nthrough the floor.\n\nElectrons belong to\na family of\nparticles called\nfermions, which obey\nPauli's exclusion\nprinciple: no two of\nthem in the same\nplace can be in\nexactly the same\nquantum state.\n\nSo in an atom they\ncan't all pile into\nthe lowest energy\nlevel. They fill the\nlevels in turn, from\nthe bottom up, and\nthe way the outer\nones are left is\nwhat gives each\nelement its\nchemistry. Without\nit, there would be\nno periodic table.\nIt also makes matter\nstiff. Push two\natoms together and\ntheir electrons\nwould have to share\nstates, which they\ncan't. Along with\nthe push of their\ncharges, that is why\nsolids take up\nspace, and why your\nhand doesn't pass\nthrough a table.\x00"""
    "optical fibers\x00"""
    "Light can be trapped\ninside a glass\nthread and sent\nacross an ocean.\n\nWhen light tries to\nleave glass at a\nshallow angle, it\ncan't: it bounces\nback in, completely.\nThis total internal\nreflection traps\nlight inside a thin\nglass fiber, which\ncarries it along,\neven around bends.\n\nFiber glass is so\npure that you could\nsee through\nkilometres of it.\nLight still fades a\nlittle, so undersea\ncables have\namplifiers every 50\nto 100 kilometres:\nshort pieces of\nfiber containing the\nelement erbium,\nwhich a laser\nenergizes to boost\nthe signal.\n\nAlmost all internet\ntraffic between\ncontinents goes\nthrough such cables\non the sea floor,\nnot satellites. A\nsingle fiber can\ncarry trillions of\nbits per second, as\nmany colors of light\nat once.\x00"""
    "every path at once\x00"""
    "In quantum physics,\nlight gets from A to\nB by taking every\npossible path at\nonce.\n\nLight bouncing off a\nmirror seems to take\nthe quickest route:\nangle in equals\nangle out. Richard\nFeynman found a\ndeeper way to see\nit. A photon\nexplores every\npossible path, even\nwild ones, and each\npath gets a little\narrow whose\ndirection depends on\nhow long that path\ntakes.\n\nAdd up all the\narrows. Far from the\nusual route,\nneighbouring paths\nhave arrows pointing\nevery which way, and\nthey cancel out.\nNear the quickest\npath, the arrows\npoint alike and add\nup. That is why\nlight seems to take\nthat path.\nYou can test it.\nScrape away parts of\na mirror far from\nthe reflection\npoint, in stripes,\nso that only arrows\npointing one way are\nleft, and light does\nreflect from there,\nat strange angles.\nThat is how a\ndiffraction grating,\nlike the rainbow\nsurface of a CD,\nworks.\x00"""
    "slow light\x00"""
    "In 1999, light was\nslowed to the speed\nof a bicycle in a\ncloud of ultracold\natoms.\n\nIn a vacuum, light\ncovers 300,000\nkilometres a second.\nIn glass or water it\nis somewhat slower.\nIn 1999, Lene Hau's\nteam sent a pulse of\nlaser light into a\nBose-Einstein\ncondensate of sodium\natoms, and it crept\nthrough at 17 metres\nper second.\n\nThe trick, called\nelectromagnetically\ninduced\ntransparency, uses a\nsecond laser to make\nthe cloud\ntransparent in a\nvery narrow range of\ncolors. There, the\nlight's energy is\nhanded back and\nforth with the atoms\nas it goes, which\nholds the pulse\nback.\n\nIn 2001, the team\nstopped a pulse\ncompletely, stored\nit in the atoms, and\nlater sent it on its\nway again. Storing\nlight like this\ncould be useful as\nmemory for quantum\ncomputers.\x00"""
    "quartz watches\x00"""
    "A quartz watch\ncounts the\nvibrations of a\ncrystal, 32,768\ntimes every second.\n\nQuartz is\npiezoelectric:\nsqueeze it and a\nvoltage appears\nacross it, and put a\nvoltage across it\nand it bends. So a\npiece of quartz in a\ncircuit can be made\nto vibrate, pushed\nby the electricity\nand pushing back.\nCut into a tiny\ntuning fork of the\nright shape, it\nrings at exactly\n32,768 times a\nsecond, very\nsteadily. That\nnumber is 2 to the\npower 15, so a chain\nof 15 circuits that\neach halve the rate\nturns it into one\ntick per second.\n\nThe same effect\nmakes the spark in\ngas lighters, where\na hammer strikes a\ncrystal and the\nvoltage jumps a gap,\nand turns sound into\nelectricity in some\nmicrophones.\x00"""
    "supernova neutrinos\x00"""
    "When a star\nexplodes, 99% of the\nenergy flies out as\nneutrinos, not\nlight.\n\nWhen a heavy star's\ncore collapses into\na neutron star, it\nreleases a vast\namount of energy in\nseconds. You might\nthink most of it\ncomes out as the\nbrilliant light we\nsee. Only about 1\npercent goes into\nthe light and the\nblast.\n\nThe other 99 percent\nescapes as\nneutrinos, made as\nprotons and\nelectrons are\ncrushed into\nneutrons. The core\nis so dense that\neven neutrinos are\ntrapped for a\nmoment, and they\nstream out over\nabout ten seconds.\n\nOn 23 February 1987,\na star exploded in a\ngalaxy next to ours,\nand three detectors\non Earth caught\nabout two dozen of\nits neutrinos, a few\nhours before its\nlight was seen. It\nconfirmed the theory\nand began neutrino\nastronomy.\x00"""
    "the casimir effect\x00"""
    "Two metal plates in\nperfect emptiness\nare pushed together\nby the vacuum\nitself.\n\nIn quantum physics,\nempty space is not\nquite empty. The\nelectromagnetic\nfield in it keeps\ntiny fluctuations\neven with no light\nat all, a bit like\nthe jiggling of\natoms at absolute\nzero.\n\nPut two metal plates\nvery close together,\nand between them\nonly the\nfluctuations that\nfit the gap can\nexist, while\noutside, all of them\ncan. The difference\npushes the plates\ntogether.\n\nHendrik Casimir\npredicted it in\n1948, and it was\nmeasured precisely\nin 1997. It is far\ntoo weak to notice\nat everyday sizes,\nbut with a gap of\nten nanometres it is\nabout as strong as\nair pressure: enough\nto make the parts of\ntiny machines stick\ntogether.\x00"""
    "band gaps\x00"""
    "The color of an LED\nis set by the size\nof an energy gap\ninside its crystal.\n\nIn a solid,\nelectrons can only\nhave energies in\ncertain bands. In a\nmetal, the top band\nis half full, so\nelectrons move\neasily: it conducts.\nIn an insulator, the\ntop band is full and\nthe next is far\nabove it, so they\ncan't move.\nSemiconductors like\nsilicon are in\nbetween: the gap to\nthe next band is\nsmall, so a little\nenergy, or a few\natoms of another\nelement mixed in,\nlets some electrons\ncross it. That makes\ntheir conduction\neasy to control, the\nbasis of every\ntransistor.\n\nIn an LED, electrons\nfall back across the\ngap, and each gives\noff a photon with\nthe energy of the\ngap. A wide gap\nmakes blue light, a\nnarrow one red or\ninfrared. The blue\nLED needed a crystal\nthat took decades to\ngrow well. It won\nthe 2014 Nobel Prize\nand made white LED\nlamps possible.\x00"""
    "maxwell's demon\x00"""
    "Erasing information\ncosts energy.\nDeleting a single\nbit must give off a\nlittle heat.\n\nIn 1867, James Clerk\nMaxwell imagined a\ntiny demon guarding\na door between two\nboxes of gas.\nLetting only fast\nmolecules through\none way and slow\nones the other, it\nwould make one side\nhot and the other\ncold without doing\nany work, breaking\nthe laws of\nthermodynamics.\n\nThe answer took a\ncentury. The demon\nmust remember each\nmolecule's speed to\ndecide, and its\nmemory fills up. In\n1961, Rolf Landauer\nshowed that erasing\na bit of memory must\nrelease a minimum\namount of heat,\nwhich pays back\nexactly what the\ndemon gained.\nSo information is\nphysical. The\nLandauer limit was\nconfirmed by\nexperiment in 2012.\nIt is tiny: today's\nchips use far more\nenergy than that per\nbit, so there is\nplenty of room to\nimprove.\x00"""
    "turbulence\x00"""
    "There's a\nmillion-dollar prize\nfor proving that the\nequations of flowing\nwater make sense.\n\nThe Navier-Stokes\nequations describe\nhow fluids like\nwater and air flow.\nEngineers use them\nevery day to design\nplanes, ships and\npipes, and they work\nbeautifully.\n\nBut nobody has\nproved that their\nsolutions always\nstay smooth: that a\nflow that starts\nsmooth can never\ndevelop a point\nwhere the speed\nbecomes infinite.\nIt's one of the\nseven Millennium\nPrize problems, with\na million dollars\nfor a proof either\nway.\n\nPart of the\ndifficulty is\nturbulence: the\nchaotic swirls in a\nrushing river or\nrising smoke, eddies\ninside eddies at\nevery size. Richard\nFeynman called\nturbulence the most\nimportant unsolved\nproblem of classical\nphysics.\x00"""
    "the josephson effect\x00"""
    "A superconducting\nsandwich turns a\nvoltage into a\nprecise frequency.\nIt defines the volt.\n\nPut a thin\ninsulating layer\nbetween two\nsuperconductors, and\nthe paired electrons\ntunnel through it as\na current, with no\nvoltage at all.\nBrian Josephson\npredicted it in\n1962, as a student,\nand won a Nobel\nPrize for it.\n\nAdd a voltage and\nthe current starts\nto oscillate, at a\nfrequency set only\nby that voltage and\nnatural constants:\nabout 484 billion\ncycles per second\nfor each millivolt.\nFrequencies can be\nmeasured extremely\nprecisely, so this\nis how the volt is\ndefined and checked.\n\nRings with such\njunctions, called\nSQUIDs, are the most\nsensitive magnetic\ndetectors known.\nThey can pick up the\nfaint magnetic\nfields made by the\nelectric currents in\na person's brain.\x00"""
    "the hot corona\x00"""
    "The Sun's outer\natmosphere is\nhundreds of times\nhotter than its\nsurface. Why?\n\nThe visible surface\nof the Sun is about\n5,500 degrees. You\nwould expect things\nto get cooler\nfarther out, away\nfrom the heat.\nInstead the corona,\nthe faint outer\natmosphere seen\nduring eclipses, is\nover a million\ndegrees.\n\nSomething must carry\nenergy up from below\nand release it\nthere. The two main\nsuspects are waves\ntravelling along the\nSun's magnetic field\nlines, and countless\ntiny explosions,\nnanoflares, where\ntangled field lines\nsnap and reconnect.\n\nNASA's Parker Solar\nProbe has been\nflying through the\ncorona since 2021,\ncloser to the Sun\nthan anything ever\nmade, to find out.\nSo far, the evidence\nsuggests both play a\npart.\x00"""
    "antimatter storms\x00"""
    "Thunderstorms fire\nbeams of gamma rays\ninto space, and even\nmake antimatter.\n\nInside a\nthundercloud, strong\nelectric fields\nspeed up free\nelectrons until they\nmove at nearly the\nspeed of light. When\nthey hit air\nmolecules, they\nknock more electrons\nloose, which speed\nup too: an\navalanche.\nThose electrons make\nbursts of gamma\nrays, the most\nenergetic kind of\nlight, lasting about\na thousandth of a\nsecond. They were\ndiscovered by\naccident in 1994, by\na satellite built to\nwatch for distant\ncosmic explosions.\nSome of the gamma\nrays are energetic\nenough to turn into\npairs of electrons\nand positrons. In\n2011, the Fermi\nspace telescope\ndetected beams of\npositrons,\nantimatter, shot up\ninto space from\nthunderstorms.\nLightning itself\nheats the air to\nabout 30,000\ndegrees, five times\nhotter than the\nSun's surface.\x00"""
    "solar sails\x00"""
    "Light has no mass,\nbut it pushes. A\nspaceship can sail\non sunlight alone.\n\nA photon has no\nmass, but it does\ncarry momentum, so\nwhen it bounces off\na mirror it gives\nthe mirror a tiny\npush. At Earth's\ndistance, sunlight\npushes on a square\nmetre of mirror with\nabout the weight of\na grain of sand.\n\nThat is tiny, but it\nnever stops, and in\nspace there is no\nfriction. In 2010,\nJapan's IKAROS probe\nunfurled a sail 20\nmetres across,\nthinner than a hair,\nand used sunlight to\nspeed up and steer\non its way past\nVenus.\nThe pressure of\nlight also pushes\nthe dust tails of\ncomets away from the\nSun, and helps hold\nup the outer layers\nof the biggest\nstars.\x00"""
    "sonoluminescence\x00"""
    "Sound can squeeze a\nbubble in water so\nhard that it flashes\nwith light.\n\nTrap a small air\nbubble in water with\na strong sound wave.\nAs the pressure\nswings, the bubble\ngrows, then\ncollapses violently,\ntens of thousands of\ntimes a second.\n\nAt the moment of\ncollapse, the gas\ninside is crushed\nand heats up so fast\nthat it gives off a\nflash of light\nlasting less than a\nbillionth of a\nsecond. Measurements\nsuggest thousands to\ntens of thousands of\ndegrees inside:\nhotter than the\nsurface of the Sun.\n\nExactly how the\nlight is made is\nstill debated. A\nshrimp does\nsomething similar:\nthe pistol shrimp\nsnaps its claw so\nfast that it makes a\ncollapsing bubble,\nwith a bang loud\nenough to stun its\nprey and a faint\nflash of light.\x00"""
    "white dwarfs\x00"""
    "Electrons can hold\nup a dead star, but\nonly up to 1.4 times\nthe mass of the Sun.\n\nWhen a star like the\nSun runs out of\nfuel, it shrinks\ninto a white dwarf:\na ball about the\nsize of the Earth,\nwith half the Sun's\nmass or more. A\nteaspoon of it\nweighs several tons.\n\nWhat holds it up\nisn't heat but the\nPauli exclusion\nprinciple: the\nelectrons are\nsqueezed so close\nthat they can't\nshare states, and\nthey push back. In\n1930, on a ship to\nEngland, the\n19-year-old\nSubrahmanyan\nChandrasekhar\nrealized that\nrelativity limits\nthis push, because\nthe electrons can't\ngo faster than\nlight.\nAbove about 1.4\ntimes the Sun's\nmass, gravity wins\nand the star\ncollapses further,\ninto a neutron star,\nor blows up. White\ndwarfs pushed past\nthe limit explode as\nsupernovae of almost\nthe same brightness,\nwhich is how dark\nenergy was found.\x00"""
    "fractional charge\x00"""
    "Electrons can team\nup into 'particles'\nthat carry a third\nof an electron's\ncharge.\n\nThe electron's\ncharge was thought\nto be the smallest\npiece of charge that\ncan move around\nfreely. Quarks have\nthirds, but they are\nalways locked inside\nprotons and\nneutrons.\n\nIn 1982, in a flat\nsheet of electrons\nin a strong magnetic\nfield and extreme\ncold, experiments\nfound quantum Hall\nsteps at fractions\nsuch as one third.\nThe electrons were\nmoving together so\nthat the ripples in\nthem, called\nquasiparticles,\nbehaved like\nparticles with\nexactly a third of\nan electron's\ncharge.\n\nNo electron is\nbroken: the dance as\na whole carries the\ncharge. These\nquasiparticles,\ncalled anyons, are\nneither fermions nor\nbosons, and some\nkinds of anyons may\none day make quantum\ncomputers that\nresist errors.\x00"""
    "no centre\x00"""
    "The Big Bang was not\nan explosion at a\npoint. It happened\neverywhere at once.\n\nIt's natural to\npicture the Big Bang\nas a bomb going off\nin empty space, with\ngalaxies flying out\nfrom a centre. But\nthere was no centre,\nand no outside.\n\nThe Big Bang theory\nsays that the whole\nof space was once\nextremely hot and\ndense, and has been\nexpanding ever\nsince. Every distant\ngalaxy moves away\nfrom every other,\nlike raisins in a\nrising loaf: from\neach raisin, the\nothers seem to move\naway, and none is\nthe centre.\n\nIf the universe is\ninfinite now, it was\nprobably infinite\nthen too, only\ndenser. The theory\ndescribes what\nhappened from a tiny\nfraction of a second\nafter the beginning.\nWhat came before, if\n'before' even means\nanything, is\nunknown.\x00"""
    "free fall\x00"""
    "Astronauts float not\nbecause gravity is\ngone, but because\nthey are always\nfalling.\n\nAt the height of the\nInternational Space\nStation, gravity is\nstill about 90\npercent as strong as\non the ground. The\nstation and the\nastronauts are\nfalling toward Earth\nall the time. They\njust move sideways\nso fast, 8\nkilometres a second,\nthat they keep\nmissing it.\n\nSince they all fall\ntogether, nothing\npresses on anything,\nand they float. In\n1907, Einstein\ncalled the\nrealization that a\nfalling person feels\nno weight the\nhappiest thought of\na lifetime.\n\nEinstein made it a\nprinciple: being in\nfree fall is exactly\nthe same as being\nfar from all\ngravity, and\nstanding on Earth is\nexactly the same as\nbeing pushed up in\nan accelerating\nrocket. From it came\ngeneral relativity,\nwhere gravity is not\na force at all, but\nthe curving of\nspacetime.\x00"""
    "gravitational waves\x00"""
    "Colliding black\nholes shake space\nitself, by less than\nthe width of a\nproton.\n\nGeneral relativity\npredicts that\naccelerating masses\nsend out ripples in\nspacetime,\ngravitational waves,\nthat stretch and\nsqueeze space as\nthey pass. Einstein\ndoubted they could\never be detected.\n\nOn 14 September\n2015, LIGO detected\nwaves from two black\nholes, each about 30\ntimes the mass of\nthe Sun, merging 1.3\nbillion light-years\naway. In the last\nfraction of a\nsecond, they gave\noff more power as\nwaves than all the\nstars in the\nobservable universe\ngive off as light.\n\nBy the time they\nreached Earth, the\nwaves stretched\nLIGO's 4-kilometre\narms by a few\nthousandths of a\nproton's width. The\ndetection won the\n2017 Nobel Prize.\nSince then, hundreds\nof mergers have been\ndetected, including\ncolliding neutron\nstars.\x00"""
    "uncertainty\x00"""
    "No particle can have\nan exact position\nand an exact speed\nat once, even in\nprinciple.\n\nHeisenberg's\nuncertainty\nprinciple is often\nexplained as\n'looking at an\nelectron knocks it'.\nThat happens, but it\nis not the real\nreason. The real\nreason is that a\nparticle is\ndescribed by a wave.\nA wave squeezed into\na tiny spot must be\nbuilt from many\nwavelengths mixed\ntogether, and in\nquantum physics,\nwavelength is\nmomentum: mass times\nspeed. So a sharp\nposition means a\nblurry momentum. And\na wave with one\nclean wavelength, a\nsharp momentum, has\nto spread out over a\nlong stretch: a\nblurry position.\n\nThe trade-off is set\nby Planck's\nconstant, which is\ntiny, so you never\nnotice it with a\nball. For an\nelectron in an atom\nit is everything: it\nis what stops the\nelectron from\nfalling into the\nnucleus.\x00"""
    "the blue sky\x00"""
    "The sky scatters\nviolet light even\nmore than blue. So\nwhy isn't it violet?\n\nSunlight is all the\ncolors mixed. The\ntiny molecules of\nthe air scatter\nshort wavelengths\nmuch more than long\nones: blue several\ntimes more than red.\nThat scattered light\nreaches you from\nevery part of the\nsky, which is why\nthe sky is blue.\nThis is Rayleigh\nscattering.\n\nViolet has an even\nshorter wavelength,\nso it scatters even\nmore. But sunlight\nhas less violet than\nblue to begin with,\nsome of it is\nabsorbed high in the\natmosphere, and our\neyes are much less\nsensitive to it.\n\nOur eyes have three\nkinds of color\nsensors, and the mix\nof light from the\nsky sets them off in\na way the brain\nreads as sky blue.\nAt sunset, the light\ncrosses so much air\nthat most of the\nblue is scattered\naway before it\nreaches you, leaving\nreds and oranges.\x00"""
    "quasicrystals\x00"""
    "Quasicrystals are\nordered but never\nrepeat. They were\nthought impossible.\n\nCrystals are built\nby repeating one\nsmall pattern, like\ntiles on a floor.\nMathematics shows\nthat such repeating\npatterns can only\nhave certain\nsymmetries: you can\ntile a floor with\nsquares or hexagons,\nbut not with\npentagons.\nIn 1982, Dan\nShechtman saw a\nmetal alloy whose\natoms had five-fold\nsymmetry. Colleagues\nmocked the idea, and\nthe famous chemist\nLinus Pauling said\nthere are no\nquasicrystals, only\nquasi-scientists.\nBut Shechtman was\nright: the atoms\nwere ordered, yet\nthe pattern never\nrepeated, like a\nPenrose tiling.\n\nIt won the 2011\nNobel Prize in\nChemistry. Natural\nquasicrystals were\nlater found in a\nmeteorite from\nRussia, and another\nin the glassy rock\nleft by the first\natomic bomb test.\x00"""
    "magenta\x00"""
    "Magenta is not in\nthe rainbow. No\nsingle wavelength of\nlight looks like it.\n\nEach pure color in a\nrainbow is light of\none wavelength, from\nred at the long end\nto violet at the\nshort end. Your eye\nsenses color with\nthree kinds of cone\ncells, roughly for\nred, green and blue.\n\nPure yellow light\nstimulates the red\nand green cones\ntogether. So does a\nmix of red and green\nlight, and your\nbrain can't tell the\ndifference. That is\nhow screens make\nevery color from\njust three.\n\nMagenta is what you\nsee when the red and\nblue cones are\nstimulated but not\nthe green. No single\nwavelength can do\nthat, since red and\nblue are at opposite\nends of the rainbow.\nSo magenta is a\ncolor the brain\ninvents, closing the\nrainbow into a\ncircle.\x00"""
    "the triple point\x00"""
    "At one exact\ntemperature and\npressure, water\nboils and freezes at\nthe same time.\n\nWhether water is\nice, liquid or vapor\ndepends on both\ntemperature and\npressure. Lower the\npressure and water\nboils at a lower\ntemperature: on top\nof Mount Everest it\nboils at about 70\ndegrees, which is\nwhy cooking there is\nslow.\n\nAt 0.01 degrees and\na pressure about 160\ntimes lower than at\nsea level, all three\nstates exist\ntogether in balance.\nThat is water's\ntriple point. In a\nsealed glass cell at\nthat point, ice,\nliquid water and\nvapor sit together,\nthe water freezing\nand boiling at once.\n\nBecause nature fixes\nit so exactly, the\ntriple point of\nwater was long used\nto define the\ntemperature scale\nitself.\x00"""
    "a flat universe\x00"""
    "On the largest\nscales, space is\nflat: huge triangles\nhave angles adding\nup to 180 degrees.\n\nGeneral relativity\nallows space itself\nto be curved. In a\nclosed universe,\nlike the surface of\na ball, the angles\nof a huge triangle\nwould add up to more\nthan 180 degrees. In\nan open one, to\nless.\n\nCosmologists\nmeasured it with the\nspots in the cosmic\nmicrowave\nbackground, whose\ntrue size is known\nfrom physics. How\nbig they look in the\nsky shows how space\nbent their light on\nthe way here. They\nlook just as big as\nthey would in flat\nspace, to within\nless than a percent.\n\nA flat universe\nneeds just the right\namount of matter and\nenergy, one of the\npuzzles inflation\nwas invented to\nexplain. It also\nsuggests, but\ndoesn't prove, that\nspace goes on\nforever.\x00"""
    "cosmic gold\x00"""
    "Much of the gold in\njewellery was forged\nwhen two neutron\nstars crashed\ntogether.\n\nStars make elements\nby fusion, from\nhelium up to iron.\nBut fusing iron uses\nup energy instead of\nreleasing it, so\nheavier elements\nneed another way:\npiling neutrons onto\nnuclei very fast,\nfaster than they can\ndecay.\nThat needs a huge\nflood of neutrons.\nIn 2017,\ngravitational wave\ndetectors caught two\nneutron stars\nspiralling together\n130 million\nlight-years away,\nand telescopes saw\nthe glow of the\nexplosion that\nfollowed, a\nkilonova. Its light\ncarried the\nfingerprints of\nfreshly made heavy\nelements.\nThat one collision\nis estimated to have\nmade several Earth\nmasses of heavy\nelements, including\ngold and platinum.\nSuch crashes, and\nperhaps rare kinds\nof supernova, are\nwhere most of the\ngold, platinum and\nuranium on Earth\ncame from.\x00"""
    "the 21 cm line\x00"""
    "A hydrogen atom\nflips about once in\n10 million years,\nand that maps our\ngalaxy.\n\nIn a hydrogen atom,\nboth the proton and\nthe electron have\nspin. When their\nspins point the same\nway, the atom has a\ntiny bit more energy\nthan when they point\nopposite ways.\n\nFlipping from one to\nthe other gives off\na radio wave 21\ncentimetres long.\nFor a lone atom,\nthis is so rare that\nit happens about\nonce in 10 million\nyears. But space\nholds so much\nhydrogen that the\nfaint radio glow is\neverywhere.\n\nCold hydrogen gas\ngives off no visible\nlight, so this radio\nline is how\nastronomers mapped\nthe spiral arms of\nour galaxy and\nmeasured how fast\ngalaxies spin, key\nevidence for dark\nmatter. They hope to\nuse it to see the\nuniverse before the\nfirst stars.\x00"""
    "superposition\x00"""
    "A quantum object can\nbe in a mix of two\nstates at once,\nuntil something\nmeasures it.\n\nA spinning coin is\nheads or tails, even\nif you don't know\nwhich yet. A quantum\nsystem is different:\nit can be in a\nsuperposition, a\nprecise blend of\nboth possibilities,\nand that blend is\nreal, not just our\nignorance.\nWe know it is real\nbecause the\npossibilities can\ninterfere with each\nother, adding up or\ncancelling out,\nwhich a hidden 'it\nwas heads all along'\ncould never do.\n\nWhen you measure it,\nyou always get one\nclean answer, heads\nor tails, with odds\nset by the blend.\nWhy measuring does\nthis is still argued\nabout. It is called\nthe measurement\nproblem, and there\nare many rival\ninterpretations of\nquantum mechanics\nthat try to explain\nit.\x00"""
    "topology in matter\x00"""
    "Some materials are\ninsulators inside\nbut always conduct\nelectricity on their\nsurface.\n\nTopology is the\nmathematics of\nshapes that ignores\ndetails. To a\ntopologist a\ndoughnut and a\ncoffee mug are the\nsame, since each has\none hole: you can't\nsmoothly turn either\ninto a ball without\ntearing it.\nIn some materials,\nthe quantum waves of\nthe electrons are\ntwisted in a way\nthat is topological\nin that sense.\nInside, the material\nis an insulator. But\nat the edge, where\nit meets the\nordinary outside\nworld, the twist has\nto undo itself, and\nthat forces a\nconducting layer to\nexist on the\nsurface.\n\nScratch the surface\nor add impurities,\nand the conducting\nlayer is still\nthere, because small\ndamage can't change\na topology. The 2016\nNobel Prize went to\nthe theorists who\nfirst understood\ntopological states\nof matter.\x00"""
    "noether's theorem\x00"""
    "Energy is conserved\nbecause the laws of\nphysics don't change\nover time.\n\nIn 1918, Emmy\nNoether published\none of the deepest\nresults in physics:\nevery symmetry of\nnature comes with\nsomething that is\nconserved.\n\nIf an experiment\nworks the same today\nas tomorrow (the\nlaws don't change\nwith time), energy\nis conserved. If it\nworks the same here\nas a metre to the\nleft (they don't\nchange from place to\nplace), momentum is\nconserved. If it\nworks the same\nfacing any\ndirection, spin, or\nangular momentum, is\nconserved.\n\nSo conservation laws\nare not separate\nrules: they are what\nsymmetries look\nlike. That explains\na surprise, too. The\nexpanding universe\ndoes change over\ntime, so there\nenergy isn't simply\nconserved, which is\nwhy light from\ndistant galaxies\nloses energy as it\nis stretched.\x00"""
    "neutrinos\x00"""
    "About 60 billion\nneutrinos from the\nSun pass through\nyour fingertip every\nsecond.\n\nNeutrinos are\nparticles with no\ncharge and an\nincredibly small\nmass, made in\nnuclear reactions.\nThe Sun's core makes\nenormous numbers of\nthem: about 60\nbillion pass through\neach square\ncentimetre of you\nevery second, day\nand night. At night,\nthey come up through\nthe Earth.\n\nThey almost never\nhit anything. They\nonly feel the weak\nforce and gravity,\nso a neutrino could\ncross a light-year\nof solid lead and\nprobably come out\nthe other side.\n\nTo catch a few,\ndetectors use\nthousands of tons of\nwater or ice, deep\nunderground, and\nwait. IceCube, at\nthe South Pole,\nwatches a whole\ncubic kilometre of\nAntarctic ice.\x00"""
    "teleportation\x00"""
    "Teleportation is\nreal: it moves a\nquantum state, not\nmatter, and never\nfaster than light.\n\nQuantum\nteleportation\ntransfers the exact\nquantum state of one\nparticle onto\nanother particle\nsomewhere else. It\nneeds an entangled\npair, shared between\nthe sender and the\nreceiver beforehand.\n\nThe sender measures\nthe particle\ntogether with their\nhalf of the pair,\nwhich destroys its\noriginal state, so\nnothing is ever\ncopied. That gives\ntwo ordinary bits,\nwhich are sent by\nany normal means.\nThe receiver uses\nthe bits to adjust\nthe other half of\nthe pair, which then\ntakes on the\noriginal state.\n\nWithout those bits,\nthe receiver's\nparticle is just\nnoise, so the state\ncan't arrive before\nthe message does.\nPhotons' states have\nbeen teleported over\n1,400 kilometres,\nbetween the ground\nand the Chinese\nsatellite Micius, in\n2017.\x00"""
    "millimetre clocks\x00"""
    "The best clocks can\ntell that time runs\nfaster a millimetre\nhigher up.\n\nGeneral relativity\nsays gravity slows\ntime: the deeper you\nare in a gravity\nwell, the slower\nyour clock. On Earth\nthe effect is tiny.\nYour head ages\nfaster than your\nfeet, by about half\na millionth of a\nsecond over a\nlifetime.\nOptical atomic\nclocks, which count\nthe vibrations of\nlight absorbed by\natoms held in\nlasers, are so\nstable that they\nwould lose less than\na second over the\nage of the universe.\nIn 2022, a team in\nColorado used one to\nmeasure the\ndifference in tick\nrate across a cloud\nof atoms just a\nmillimetre tall.\n\nClocks like these\ncould one day map\nthe ground beneath\nthem, or find hidden\nmasses underground,\njust by how fast\nthey tick. The\nsecond will probably\nsoon be redefined\nwith them.\x00"""
    "the higgs field\x00"""
    "The Higgs field\ngives particles\ntheir mass, yet it\nexplains only about\n1% of yours.\n\nSpace is filled with\nthe Higgs field.\nParticles like\nelectrons and quarks\ninteract with it,\nand that is what\ngives them mass: the\nstronger the\ninteraction, the\nheavier the\nparticle. A ripple\nin the field, the\nHiggs boson, was\nfound at CERN in\n2012.\n\nBut the quarks in\nyour protons and\nneutrons are very\nlight. Together they\nmake up only about 1\npercent of a\nproton's mass, and\nelectrons are\nlighter still.\n\nThe rest is energy.\nThe quarks are held\ntogether by gluons\nin a furious,\never-changing swarm,\nand by Einstein's E\n= mc^2, that energy\nweighs. So almost\nall of your weight\nis the energy of the\nstrong force,\ntrapped inside the\nnuclei of your\natoms.\x00"""
    "gravitational lenses\x00"""
    "Gravity bends light.\nWhole galaxies act\nas lenses that\nmagnify what is\nbehind them.\n\nGeneral relativity\nsays mass curves\nspace and time, and\nlight follows the\ncurve. In 1919,\nduring a solar\neclipse, Arthur\nEddington's team\nmeasured starlight\npassing near the Sun\nbent by the amount\nEinstein predicted,\nwhich made Einstein\nworld-famous\novernight.\n\nA big cluster of\ngalaxies bends the\nlight from galaxies\nfar behind it,\nmagnifying them and\nsmearing them into\narcs. When the\nline-up is perfect,\nthe background\ngalaxy appears as a\nring around the\ncluster: an Einstein\nring.\n\nAstronomers use\nthese natural\ntelescopes to see\nsome of the most\ndistant galaxies,\nand to weigh the\nclusters, which is\none of the ways we\nknow dark matter is\nthere. One lensed\nsupernova was seen\nfour times at once,\nits light arriving\nalong four different\npaths.\x00"""
    "spintronics\x00"""
    "Hard drives grew\nhuge thanks to a\nquantum effect in\nmetal layers a few\natoms thick.\n\nElectrons have spin,\nwhich makes them\ntiny magnets. In a\nmagnetic metal,\nelectrons spinning\none way pass through\nmore easily than\nthose spinning the\nother way.\n\nIn 1988, two teams\nin France and\nGermany stacked\nmagnetic layers a\nfew atoms thick with\nnon-magnetic metal\nbetween them. When\nthe magnetic layers\npointed the same\nway, current flowed\neasily. When they\npointed opposite\nways, the resistance\nshot up. A tiny\nmagnetic field could\nswitch between the\ntwo.\n\nThis giant\nmagnetoresistance\nmade it possible to\nread much smaller\nmagnetic bits, and\nwithin ten years\nnearly every hard\ndrive used it. It\nwon the 2007 Nobel\nPrize and started\nspintronics:\nelectronics that\nuses spin as well as\ncharge.\x00"""
    "quark-gluon plasma\x00"""
    "Smashing gold nuclei\ntogether made a\nliquid of quarks,\nthe hottest stuff\never made.\n\nFor the first\nmillionths of a\nsecond after the Big\nBang, the universe\nwas too hot for\nprotons and neutrons\nto exist. Their\nquarks and gluons\nroamed freely, in a\nsoup called\nquark-gluon plasma.\n\nColliders recreate\nit by smashing heavy\nnuclei, like gold or\nlead, together at\nnearly the speed of\nlight. For a tiny\ninstant, the\ncollision reaches\ntrillions of\ndegrees: in 2012,\nthe LHC reached\nabout 5 trillion\ndegrees, a record.\n\nPhysicists expected\na gas. Instead the\nplasma flows like a\nliquid, with nearly\nthe least internal\nfriction that\nquantum physics\nallows: one of the\nmost perfect liquids\nknown.\x00"""
    "magnetars\x00"""
    "A magnetar is a\nmagnet so strong it\ncould wreck your\natoms from 1,000 km\naway.\n\nA magnetar is a\nneutron star with\nthe strongest\nmagnetic field\nknown: up to a\nthousand trillion\ntimes Earth's, and\nabout a billion\ntimes stronger than\nthe strongest steady\nmagnet ever made in\na lab.\nAt that strength,\nmagnetism deforms\natoms themselves,\nstretching their\nelectron clouds into\nthin needles along\nthe field. Within\nabout a thousand\nkilometres, it would\nbe deadly, tearing\napart the chemistry\nof the molecules in\nyour body.\n\nMagnetars have\nstarquakes, as their\ncrust cracks under\nthe strain. In 2004,\na flare from one\n50,000 light-years\naway was so bright\nthat, for a fraction\nof a second, it\nmeasurably disturbed\nEarth's upper\natmosphere.\x00"""
    "the kilogram\x00"""
    "Since 2019, the\nkilogram is set by\nquantum physics, not\na metal lump near\nParis.\n\nFor 130 years, the\nkilogram was a\ncylinder of platinum\nand iridium kept in\na vault near Paris.\nEvery scale in the\nworld was ultimately\nchecked against it.\nBut it seemed to\nchange slowly, by\nabout 50 micrograms\ncompared to its\ncopies, and nobody\ncould say which ones\nwere drifting.\n\nSince 2019, the\nkilogram is defined\nby fixing the value\nof Planck's\nconstant, the number\nat the heart of\nquantum physics. A\nKibble balance\nweighs a mass\nagainst an\nelectromagnetic\nforce, measured with\nquantum effects in\nelectronics, which\nties the mass to\nPlanck's constant.\n\nNow all seven base\nunits, like the\nmetre and the\nsecond, are defined\nby constants of\nnature. In\nprinciple, aliens\ncould rebuild our\nkilogram exactly\nfrom a description\nalone.\x00"""
    "brownian motion\x00"""
    "Grains jiggling in\nwater are how\nEinstein proved that\natoms are real.\n\nIn 1827, the\nbotanist Robert\nBrown saw tiny\nparticles from\npollen jiggle\nendlessly in water\nunder a microscope,\neven ones from\nlong-dead plants.\nNobody could explain\nit.\n\nIn 1905, Einstein\nworked out that if\nwater is made of\nmolecules too small\nto see, always\nmoving, then a small\ngrain would be\nbumped unevenly from\ndifferent sides and\nwander in a random\nzigzag. Einstein\npredicted how far it\nshould drift,\ndepending on the\nsize of the\nmolecules.\n\nJean Perrin measured\nit and confirmed the\nprediction, which\nwon the 1926 Nobel\nPrize. Until then,\nmany respected\nscientists doubted\nthat atoms were\nreal. The same\nmathematics now\ndescribes stock\nprices and how heat\nspreads.\x00"""
    "seeing atoms\x00"""
    "A needle with a\nsingle atom at its\ntip can feel and\neven move single\natoms, by tunneling.\n\nAtoms are far too\nsmall to see with\nlight, whose waves\nare thousands of\ntimes bigger. The\nscanning tunneling\nmicroscope, invented\nin 1981, finds them\nby touch instead.\n\nA metal needle, so\nsharp it ends in a\nsingle atom, is\nbrought within a\nnanometre of a\nsurface. Electrons\ntunnel across the\ngap, and the current\nchanges enormously\nwith distance: about\ntenfold for every\ntenth of a\nnanometre. Scanning\nthe needle while\nkeeping the current\nsteady traces the\nbumps of the\nindividual atoms.\n\nThe needle can also\npick atoms up and\nset them down. In\n1989, IBM\nresearchers spelled\nthe company's name\nwith 35 xenon atoms,\nand later made a\nshort film, A Boy\nand His Atom, by\nmoving single atoms\naround.\x00"""
    "gps and relativity\x00"""
    "Without Einstein's\nrelativity, GPS\nwould be kilometres\noff within a day.\n\nGPS satellites carry\natomic clocks, and\nyour phone finds\nwhere it is by\ntiming signals from\nseveral of them.\nLight covers 30\ncentimetres in a\nbillionth of a\nsecond, so the\nclocks must agree to\nwithin a few\nbillionths of a\nsecond.\n\nRelativity changes\ntheir rate in two\nways. They move\nfast, which by\nspecial relativity\nmakes them tick\nslower, losing about\n7 microseconds a\nday. But they are\nhigh up, where\ngravity is weaker,\nand by general\nrelativity clocks\nthere tick faster,\ngaining about 45\nmicroseconds a day.\nTogether that's 38\nmicroseconds a day\ntoo fast. Left\nalone, positions\nwould drift by about\n10 kilometres every\nday. So the\nsatellite clocks are\nset to tick slightly\nslow before launch,\nby exactly the\namount Einstein's\nequations give.\x00"""
    "quantum tunneling\x00"""
    "Particles can pass\nstraight through\nwalls they don't\nhave the energy to\nclimb over.\n\nThrow a ball at a\nwall without enough\nspeed to get over\nit, and it bounces\nback. Every time.\nBut an electron is\nnot quite a ball:\nquantum physics\ndescribes it with a\nwave that says where\nit is likely to be\nfound.\nThat wave does not\nstop dead at a\nbarrier. It fades\naway inside it, and\nif the barrier is\nthin enough, a\nlittle of it is\nstill there on the\nfar side. So there\nis a small chance\nthat the particle\nsimply turns up on\nthe other side,\nwithout ever having\nhad the energy to go\nover. This is\ntunneling.\n\nIt is not rare in\npractice. The flash\nmemory in phones and\nUSB sticks stores\nbits by tunneling\nelectrons through a\nthin insulating\nlayer, and some\nradioactive atoms\ndecay by letting a\npiece of their\nnucleus tunnel out.\x00"""
    "attosecond flashes\x00"""
    "Flashes of light\nlasting a billionth\nof a billionth of a\nsecond can film\nelectrons moving.\n\nTo photograph\nsomething fast, you\nneed a short flash.\nElectrons move in\natoms over\nattoseconds:\nbillionths of a\nbillionth of a\nsecond. There are\nmore attoseconds in\none second than\nthere have been\nseconds since the\nBig Bang.\n\nVisible light can't\nmake a flash that\nshort, since a\nsingle one of its\nwaves lasts longer.\nPhysicists found a\nway to make bursts\nof extreme\nultraviolet light\nlasting a few\nhundred attoseconds,\nby hitting a gas\nwith an intense\nlaser that tears\nelectrons away and\nslams them back into\ntheir atoms.\n\nWith these flashes,\nit is possible to\nwatch electrons move\nin atoms and\nmolecules, and time\nhow long one takes\nto escape. The\nmethod won the 2023\nNobel Prize.\x00"""
    "the weak force\x00"""
    "The weak force can\nturn one particle\ninto another.\nWithout it, the Sun\nwouldn't shine.\n\nOf the four forces\nof nature, the weak\nforce is the odd\none. It doesn't just\npush or pull: it can\nchange a particle's\nidentity, turning a\nneutron into a\nproton, or the\nreverse.\n\nThe Sun needs this.\nIts fuel is protons,\nbut helium needs\nneutrons too, so in\nthe first step of\nfusion two protons\nmeet and one of them\nmust turn into a\nneutron by the weak\nforce. That is so\nunlikely that a\nproton in the Sun's\ncore waits billions\nof years, on\naverage, for it to\nhappen.\n\nThat slowness is\nlucky: it is why the\nSun burns steadily\nfor ten billion\nyears instead of\nexploding at once.\nThe weak force is\ncarried by the heavy\nW and Z particles,\nwhich is why it only\nreaches across a\ntiny fraction of a\nproton's width.\x00"""
    "the butterfly effect\x00"""
    "Weather can't be\nforecast more than\nabout two weeks\nahead, even with\nperfect computers.\n\nIn 1961, Edward\nLorenz reran a\nweather simulation,\ntyping in a number\nrounded to three\ndecimals instead of\nsix. The tiny\ndifference grew\nuntil the new\nforecast had nothing\nin common with the\nold one.\nThat is chaos. In\nsome systems that\nfollow exact laws,\nsmall differences\ngrow exponentially,\ndoubling again and\nagain. A system can\nbe completely\ndetermined by its\nlaws and still\nunpredictable,\nbecause we can never\nmeasure its starting\npoint perfectly.\n\nIn the atmosphere,\nsmall errors double\nevery couple of\ndays, which limits\ndetailed forecasts\nto roughly two\nweeks. Lorenz\ndescribed it by\nasking whether the\nflap of a\nbutterfly's wings in\nBrazil could set off\na tornado in Texas.\x00"""
    "the second\x00"""
    "A second is exactly\n9,192,631,770 waves\nof the light a\ncaesium atom\nabsorbs.\n\nAtoms of the same\nelement are all\nidentical, and a\njump between two of\ntheir energy levels\nabsorbs light of one\nprecise frequency,\nthe same everywhere\nand forever. That\nmakes them perfect\npendulums.\n\nSince 1967, the\nsecond is defined by\ncaesium-133: it\nlasts 9,192,631,770\ncycles of the\nmicrowaves that make\nits outer electron\nflip its spin\nrelative to the\nnucleus. An atomic\nclock locks a\nmicrowave source to\nthat frequency, and\ncounts.\n\nThe best caesium\nclocks would lose\nabout a second in\n300 million years.\nNewer optical\nclocks, ticking with\nvisible light, are\nabout a hundred\ntimes better, and\nthe second will\nprobably be\nredefined with them.\x00"""
    "antimatter\x00"""
    "Every particle has\nan antimatter twin,\nand a banana gives\noff a little\nantimatter.\n\nFor each kind of\nparticle there is an\nantiparticle with\nthe same mass and\nthe opposite charge.\nThe electron's twin\nis the positron.\nWhen a particle\nmeets its\nantiparticle, both\nvanish in a flash of\nenergy.\nAntimatter is not\nonly made in labs. A\nbanana contains a\nlittle radioactive\npotassium-40, and\nabout one decay in a\nhundred thousand\ngives off a\npositron: one every\nhour or two. It\nmeets an electron at\nonce and turns into\ntwo gamma rays.\n\nHospitals use this\nin PET scans. A\nsugar labelled with\na substance that\ngives off positrons\nis injected, the\ncells using the most\nsugar light up, and\nthe pairs of gamma\nrays, flying out in\nopposite directions,\nshow exactly where.\x00"""
    "holograms\x00"""
    "A hologram records\nnot only how bright\nlight is, but also\nhow its waves line\nup.\n\nA photograph records\nhow bright the light\nis at each point,\nwhich isn't enough\nto rebuild a 3D\nscene. Light also\nhas phase: where\neach wave is in its\nup-and-down cycle,\nwhich depends on how\nfar it has\ntravelled.\nTo record a\nhologram, laser\nlight is split in\ntwo: one beam lights\nthe object, the\nother goes straight\nto the film. Where\nthey meet, they\ninterfere, and the\nfilm records a\npattern of fine\nstripes that encodes\nthe phase.\n\nShine a laser back\nthrough the film and\nthe stripes bend the\nlight into the very\nsame waves that came\nfrom the object.\nYour eyes can't tell\nthe difference, so\nyou see the object\nin 3D, and moving\nyour head shows its\nsides. Cut the\nhologram in half and\neach half still\nshows the whole\nscene.\x00"""
    "the hologram idea\x00"""
    "What a black hole\nholds depends on its\narea, not its\nvolume. Space may be\na hologram.\n\nNormally, how much\ninformation you can\nstore in a region\ngrows with its\nvolume: make it\ntwice as big in each\ndirection and you\nget eight times the\nroom. Black holes\nbreak that rule.\n\nIn the 1970s, Jacob\nBekenstein and\nStephen Hawking\nshowed that a black\nhole has entropy, a\nmeasure of hidden\ninformation, in\nproportion to the\narea of its horizon:\nabout one bit for\nevery few squared\nPlanck lengths. And\na black hole is the\nmost information\nthat can ever be\nsqueezed into a\nregion.\n\nThis suggests that\neverything inside a\nregion of space\nmight be fully\ndescribed by\ninformation on its\nboundary, like a 3D\nimage stored on a\nflat hologram. In\nsome model\nuniverses,\nphysicists have\nshown exactly that.\nIt is one of the\nmost studied ideas\nin quantum gravity.\x00"""
    "decoherence\x00"""
    "Big things don't act\nquantum because the\nworld around them is\nalways measuring\nthem.\n\nIf an electron can\nbe in two places at\nonce, why not a cat?\nThe best answer\nphysics has is\ndecoherence.\nA superposition only\nshows while its\npossibilities can\ninterfere. But a cat\nis hit by air\nmolecules, light and\nheat every instant,\nand each collision\nrecords where it is.\nThe information\nleaks into the\nsurroundings, and\nthe interference\nbecomes impossible\nto see, in far less\nthan a trillionth of\na second.\n\nThat is why quantum\ncomputers must be\nkept so cold and\nisolated: they are\nfighting\ndecoherence.\nExperiments have\npushed the limit up\nto molecules of\nabout 2,000 atoms,\nwhich have been sent\nthrough two slits at\nonce and interfered\nwith themselves.\x00"""
    "the uv catastrophe\x00"""
    "Old physics said a\nhot oven should glow\nwith infinite\nenergy. Quanta fixed\nit.\n\nEverything warm\nglows: you give off\ninfrared, a stove\nring glows red, the\nSun white. Around\n1900, physicists\ntried to calculate\nthe mix of colors in\nthis glow, called\nblack-body\nradiation.\n\nClassical physics\npredicted the glow\nshould get brighter\nand brighter at\nshorter wavelengths,\nwithout end, so any\nhot object would\nblast out infinite\nultraviolet energy.\nThat obviously\ndoesn't happen. It\nbecame known as the\nultraviolet\ncatastrophe.\n\nIn 1900, Max Planck\nfound a formula that\nmatched experiments\nperfectly, but only\nby assuming that\nenergy is given out\nin small packets,\nquanta, whose size\ngrows with\nfrequency. Planck\ntook it as a\nmathematical trick.\nIt was the beginning\nof quantum physics.\x00"""
    "microwave ovens\x00"""
    "Microwave ovens\ndon't work by\nmatching a special\nvibration of water.\nThat's a myth.\n\nIt is often said\nthat microwaves are\ntuned to water's\nresonant frequency.\nIn fact, ovens run\nat 2.45 gigahertz,\nfar below water's\nstrongest\nabsorption, which is\nat much higher\nfrequencies.\n\nWater molecules are\nlopsided: one end\nslightly negative,\nthe other slightly\npositive. The\nmicrowave's electric\nfield flips billions\nof times a second,\nand the molecules\nkeep trying to twist\nto follow it,\njostling their\nneighbours and\nturning that motion\ninto heat. Many\nfrequencies would\nwork.\n\n2.45 GHz was chosen\npartly because that\nband was left free\nfor such uses, and\nbecause it isn't\nabsorbed too\nstrongly, so the\nwaves soak a few\ncentimetres into the\nfood instead of only\nheating its surface.\nIn ice, the\nmolecules can't\ntwist freely, which\nis why defrosting is\nslow.\x00"""
    "the bird compass\x00"""
    "Birds may sense the\nEarth's magnetic\nfield with linked\nelectrons in their\neyes.\n\nMany migrating birds\ncan tell direction\nfrom Earth's\nmagnetic field, even\non cloudy nights.\nBut the field is\nvery weak, far too\nweak to tug\nnoticeably on any\nordinary molecule.\nHow they do it is\nstill being worked\nout.\n\nThe leading idea\ninvolves a protein\nin their eyes called\ncryptochrome. When\nblue light hits it,\nit makes a pair of\nelectrons on two\ndifferent molecules\nwhose spins are\nlinked, a radical\npair. The Earth's\nfield slightly\nchanges how the\npair's spins evolve,\nwhich changes the\nchemicals the\nprotein ends up\nmaking.\n\nIf this is right,\nbirds use a delicate\nquantum effect in a\nwarm, wet body,\nwhich many thought\nimpossible. Weak\nradio noise disrupts\nthe compass of\nrobins, as the\ntheory predicts, but\nthe full story isn't\nproven yet.\x00"""
    "falling into a hole\x00"""
    "If you watched a\nfriend fall into a\nblack hole, you\nwould never see them\ncross the edge.\n\nNear a black hole,\ngravity slows time\nenormously. As your\nfriend falls toward\nthe event horizon,\nthe point of no\nreturn, their clock\nlooks slower and\nslower to you, and\ntheir light gets\nredder and dimmer.\n\nFrom far away, you'd\nsee them slow down\nand fade at the\nhorizon, never quite\ncrossing it, until\ntheir light is too\nfaint to detect.\n\nFor your friend,\nnothing special\nhappens at the\nhorizon: they cross\nit in a finite time\nand keep falling.\nNear a black hole a\nfew times the mass\nof the Sun, its\ntides would stretch\nthem apart first:\nspaghettification. A\ngiant one, like\nthose at the centres\nof galaxies, is so\nlarge that they\nwould cross the\nhorizon unharmed.\x00"""
    "the leaving moon\x00"""
    "The Moon moves 3.8\ncm farther from\nEarth every year,\nand our days get\nlonger.\n\nThe Moon's gravity\nraises tides on\nEarth. Earth spins\nfaster than the Moon\norbits, so the bulge\nof the tides is\ncarried slightly\nahead of the Moon,\nand its pull tugs\nthe Moon forward,\nlifting it into a\nhigher orbit.\nIn return, the\nMoon's pull on the\nbulge brakes Earth's\nspin. Our day grows\nlonger by about 2\nthousandths of a\nsecond every\ncentury. 400 million\nyears ago, a year\nhad about 400 days,\nwhich can be counted\nin the daily growth\nlines of fossil\ncorals.\n\nWe know the Moon's\ndistance so\nprecisely because\nastronauts left\nmirrors on it.\nObservatories fire\nlasers at them and\ntime the echo, to\nwithin millimetres.\x00"""
    "left and right\x00"""
    "Nature can tell left\nfrom right. In 1956\nan experiment caught\nit doing so.\n\nFor a long time\nphysicists assumed\nthat the laws of\nnature work the same\nin a mirror:\nanything that can\nhappen, its mirror\nimage could happen\njust as well.\n\nIn 1956,\nChien-Shiung Wu\ntested it with\nradioactive\ncobalt-60, cooled\nclose to absolute\nzero and lined up in\na magnetic field. If\nnature didn't care\nabout mirrors, the\nelectrons from its\ndecay would fly out\nequally along and\nagainst the spin of\nthe nuclei. They\ndidn't: they\npreferred one\ndirection.\n\nThe weak force\nbreaks mirror\nsymmetry. It only\nacts on particles\nspinning one way\nrelative to their\nmotion, the\nleft-handed ones.\nEvery neutrino ever\nseen is left-handed,\nand every\nantineutrino\nright-handed. Why\nnature is built like\nthis, nobody knows.\x00"""
    "the tunneling sun\x00"""
    "The Sun shines only\nbecause protons\ntunnel through a\nwall they can't\nclimb over.\n\nIn the Sun's core,\nprotons must get\nvery close to fuse.\nBut they are all\npositively charged,\nand repel each\nother. Even at 15\nmillion degrees, a\ntypical proton has\nabout a thousand\ntimes too little\nenergy to get over\nthat electric wall.\n\nSo by classical\nphysics, the Sun\nshouldn't shine at\nall. What saves it\nis quantum\ntunneling: there is\na tiny chance that\ntwo protons pass\nthrough the barrier\ninstead of over it.\nThe chance is tiny\nfor each collision,\nbut the core holds\nan astronomical\nnumber of protons,\ncolliding all the\ntime. The numbers\nwork out to the\nSun's real\nbrightness. It was\none of the first\ntriumphs of quantum\nphysics in the\nstars, worked out by\nGeorge Gamow and\nothers in the late\n1920s.\x00"""
    "the false vacuum\x00"""
    "Our universe may be\nonly temporarily\nstable, like a ball\nresting in a dip on\na hillside.\n\nThe Higgs field\nfills space with a\ncertain value, which\nsets the masses of\nthe particles.\nMeasurements of the\nHiggs boson and the\ntop quark suggest\nthis might not be\nthe lowest possible\nvalue, just a local\ndip: a false vacuum.\nIf so, a bubble of\nthe true vacuum\ncould one day appear\nsomewhere by quantum\ntunneling, and grow\nat nearly the speed\nof light, changing\nthe laws of\nchemistry inside it.\n\nDon't worry.\nCalculations put the\nchance of that at\nessentially zero for\nfar longer than the\nage of the universe.\nAnd it depends on\nphysics we may not\nknow yet, which\ncould make our\nvacuum perfectly\nstable.\x00"""
    "the coriolis myth\x00"""
    "Sinks do not drain\nthe other way in the\nother hemisphere.\nThat's a myth.\n\nThe Earth's spin\nmakes moving things\nseem to curve: to\nthe right in the\nnorth, to the left\nin the south. This\nCoriolis effect is\nwhy hurricanes spin\nopposite ways in the\ntwo hemispheres.\n\nBut it is very weak\nover small distances\nand short times. In\na sink, the way the\nwater was poured,\nthe shape of the\nbasin and leftover\ncurrents matter\nthousands of times\nmore than the\nEarth's rotation.\n\nIn careful\nexperiments with a\nbig round tank left\nto settle for a day,\nthe Coriolis effect\ndoes win, as shown\nin 1962 in the US\nand in 1965 in\nAustralia. A\nFoucault pendulum, a\nheavy weight on a\nlong wire, shows the\nsame effect, slowly\nturning its swing\nduring the day.\x00"""
    "cherenkov light\x00"""
    "Particles can outrun\nlight in water. The\nresult is a blue\nglow, a sonic boom\nof light.\n\nNothing goes faster\nthan light in a\nvacuum. But light\nslows down in water,\nto about three\nquarters of its top\nspeed, and particles\nfrom a nuclear\nreactor can go\nfaster than that.\n\nA charged particle\nmoving through water\ndisturbs the\nmolecules it passes,\nand they give off\nlight. Normally\nthose little waves\ncancel out. But when\nthe particle outruns\nthe light, the waves\npile up into a cone,\nlike the shock wave\nof a plane flying\nfaster than sound.\n\nThat is Cherenkov\nradiation, the eerie\nblue glow in the\nwater around a\nreactor core.\nNeutrino detectors\nuse it too: a\nneutrino that does\nhit something makes\na fast particle,\nwhose cone of light\nreveals which way it\ncame.\x00"""
    "confinement\x00"""
    "You can never pull a\nquark out of a\nproton. Try, and you\nmake new quarks\ninstead.\n\nProtons and neutrons\nare made of three\nquarks each, held by\nthe strong force,\nwhich is carried by\nparticles called\ngluons. Unlike\ngravity or\nmagnetism, this\nforce does not get\nweaker as the quarks\nmove apart.\nPull two quarks\napart and the field\nbetween them forms a\nnarrow tube, like a\nstretched rubber\nband, holding more\nand more energy.\nLong before a quark\ngets free, there is\nenough energy in the\nband to make a new\nquark and antiquark\n(E = mc^2 again).\nThe band snaps, and\neach end grabs one\nof the new quarks.\n\nSo you end up with\ntwo particles made\nof quarks, never a\nlone quark. Nobody\nhas ever seen one\nalone. Proving\nmathematically why\nthe theory behaves\nlike this is part of\none of the\nMillennium Prize\nproblems, worth a\nmillion dollars.\x00"""
    "old sunlight\x00"""
    "The light you see\nfrom the Sun left\nits core tens of\nthousands of years\nago.\n\nEnergy is made in\nthe Sun's core by\nfusion, as gamma\nrays. But the Sun's\ninside is so dense\nthat light only\ntravels a tiny\ndistance, less than\na centimetre, before\nit hits a particle\nand is sent off in a\nrandom direction.\nSo energy doesn't\nstream out: it\nstaggers, like\nsomeone lost in a\ncrowd, absorbed and\ngiven off again and\nagain, slowly\nturning into\nlower-energy light.\nEstimates of how\nlong it takes to\nreach the surface\nrange from about ten\nthousand to over a\nhundred thousand\nyears.\n\nFrom the surface, it\nreaches Earth in\njust over 8 minutes.\nThe sunlight warming\nyou today began its\njourney before the\nend of the last ice\nage.\x00"""
    "slippery ice\x00"""
    "Nobody fully agrees\nwhy ice is slippery.\nThe old textbook\nanswer is wrong.\n\nFor a long time,\nschools taught that\nthe pressure of a\nskate's blade melts\nthe ice under it,\nmaking a slippery\nfilm of water. But\nthe numbers don't\nwork: a skater's\nweight lowers the\nmelting point by\nonly a few\nhundredths of a\ndegree, and ice is\nslippery even far\nbelow zero, when\nyou're just standing\non it.\n\nAnother idea is\nfriction: rubbing\nwarms the ice and\nmelts it. That\nmatters when you're\nmoving fast. But a\nbig part seems to be\nthe surface itself:\nthe top layers of\nmolecules in ice are\nnot locked into the\ncrystal like those\nbelow, and behave\nsomewhat like a\nliquid, even far\nbelow freezing.\n\nRecent experiments\nsuggest even this is\nnot the whole story,\nand that how the\nsurface molecules\nroll and tumble\nunder a skate\nmatters too.\nSomething as\neveryday as skating\nis still being\nresearched.\x00"""
    "spin\x00"""
    "Turn an electron a\nfull circle and it\nis not back where it\nstarted. It takes\ntwo turns.\n\nElectrons have spin:\nthey act like tiny\nmagnets, as if they\nwere spinning,\nalthough they are\npoints and nothing\nis actually turning.\nSpin is a quantum\nproperty with no\neveryday twin.\n\nOne strange thing\nabout it: rotate an\nelectron's state by\na full 360 degrees\nand its wave comes\nback with its sign\nflipped, minus where\nit was plus. Only\nafter 720 degrees is\nit truly the same\nagain.\n\nA flipped sign\nsounds invisible,\nbut interference can\nreveal it. In 1975,\nexperiments split\nbeams of neutrons,\nwhich have the same\nkind of spin, turned\none beam a full\ncircle with a\nmagnetic field, and\nsaw the two beams\ncancel where they\nused to add up.\x00"""
    "squeezed light\x00"""
    "To hear\ngravitational waves,\nLIGO had to quiet\nthe jitter of empty\nspace itself.\n\nLIGO measures\nchanges in length\nsmaller than a\nthousandth of a\nproton, with laser\nlight bouncing\nbetween mirrors 4\nkilometres apart. At\nthat level, even the\nlight is a problem:\nphotons arrive at\nrandom moments, like\nrain on a roof,\nwhich makes a faint\nhiss.\n\nThe uncertainty\nprinciple sets a\nlimit on that noise,\nbut only on the\nproduct of two\nthings: the jitter\nin the light's\nbrightness and the\njitter in its\ntiming, its phase.\nYou can make one\nsmaller, if you\naccept more of the\nother.\nSince 2019, LIGO\nfeeds in squeezed\nlight: a special\nstate of the vacuum\nwhose timing jitter\nis smaller than\nnormal, with the\nextra uncertainty\npushed into\nbrightness. It lets\nthe detectors hear\nfurther into space,\nand catch more black\nholes colliding.\x00"""
    "helium\x00"""
    "Helium was found on\nthe Sun 27 years\nbefore anyone found\nit on Earth.\n\nEach element absorbs\nand gives off light\nonly at its own\nparticular colors,\nlike a barcode,\nbecause its\nelectrons can only\njump between certain\nenergy levels. Split\nlight into a rainbow\nand these barcodes\nshow as sharp lines.\nThis is\nspectroscopy.\n\nDuring a solar\neclipse in 1868,\nastronomers saw a\nbright yellow line\nin the light from\nthe Sun's edge that\nmatched no known\nelement. Norman\nLockyer named the\nnew element helium,\nafter Helios, the\nGreek word for the\nSun.\n\nIn 1895 it was\nfinally found on\nEarth, released from\na uranium mineral.\nSpectroscopy is\nstill how we know\nwhat stars, and the\natmospheres of\nplanets around other\nstars, are made of,\nwithout ever going\nthere.\x00"""
    "the first minutes\x00"""
    "Almost all the\nhelium in the\nuniverse was made in\nits first few\nminutes.\n\nIn its first second,\nthe universe was a\nhot soup of protons,\nneutrons, electrons\nand light. As it\nexpanded and cooled,\nafter a few minutes,\nprotons and neutrons\ncould finally stick\ntogether without\nbeing blasted apart\nagain.\nFor a few minutes,\nthe whole universe\nwas a fusion\nreactor. Almost all\nthe neutrons ended\nup in helium, until\nthe universe became\ntoo cold and thin to\nfuse. It stopped\nwith about 75\npercent hydrogen and\n25 percent helium by\nmass, and a trace of\nlithium.\n\nThose amounts match\nwhat we measure in\nthe oldest stars and\ngas clouds, one of\nthe strongest pieces\nof evidence for the\nBig Bang. Everything\nheavier, the carbon\nin you and the\noxygen you breathe,\nwas made much later,\ninside stars.\x00"""
    "frame dragging\x00"""
    "The spinning Earth\ndrags space and time\naround with it, very\nslightly.\n\nGeneral relativity\nsays a spinning mass\ndoesn't just curve\nspacetime, it twists\nit, dragging it\naround like a spoon\nturning in honey.\nThis is frame\ndragging.\n\nIn 2004 and 2005,\nthe Gravity Probe B\nsatellite orbited\nEarth with four\ngyroscopes: quartz\nspheres among the\nroundest objects\never made, spinning\nin a near-perfect\nvacuum. Any drift in\nwhere they pointed\nwould have to come\nfrom spacetime\nitself.\n\nTheir axes drifted\nby the amount\nEinstein's theory\npredicts. The\nframe-dragging part\nwas tiny: about the\nwidth of a hair seen\nfrom 500 metres\naway, per year. Near\nspinning black\nholes, frame\ndragging is huge:\nclose enough,\nnothing can stand\nstill, and\neverything is swept\naround.\x00"""
    "no-cloning\x00"""
    "It is impossible to\ncopy an unknown\nquantum state. The\nlaws of physics\nforbid it.\n\nYou can copy a\ncomputer file as\noften as you like.\nBut in 1982,\nphysicists proved\nthat no machine,\nhowever clever, can\ntake an unknown\nquantum state and\nturn out two perfect\ncopies of it.\n\nMeasuring a state to\nlearn it disturbs\nit, and the rules of\nquantum physics\ndon't allow any\ncopier that works\nfor every possible\ninput.\n\nThat is the basis of\nquantum\ncryptography. If a\nsecret key is sent\nas single photons, a\nspy can't copy them\nto read later. Any\nattempt to look\ndisturbs them, the\ntwo people talking\ncan notice the\ndisturbance, and\nthey throw that key\naway.\x00"""
    "quantum dots\x00"""
    "A quantum dot is a\ncrystal so tiny that\nits size, not its\nmaterial, sets its\ncolor.\n\nA quantum dot is a\nspeck of\nsemiconductor only a\nfew nanometres\nacross: a few\nthousand atoms. That\nis so small that the\nelectrons inside\nfeel the walls of\nthe crystal around\nthem.\n\nIn quantum physics,\nan electron trapped\nin a small space can\nonly have certain\nenergies, like a\nguitar string that\ncan only play\ncertain notes. The\nsmaller the space,\nthe further apart\nthose energies are.\n\nWhen an electron\ndrops from a higher\nenergy to a lower\none, it gives off\nlight, and the color\ndepends on the size\nof the drop. So a\nsmall dot glows\nblue, and a bigger\ndot of the very same\nmaterial glows red.\n\nQuantum dots are\nused in QLED TV\nscreens and to make\ncells glow under a\nmicroscope.\nDiscovering and\nmaking them won the\n2023 Nobel Prize in\nChemistry.\x00"""
    "photoelectric effect\x00"""
    "Einstein's Nobel\nPrize wasn't for\nrelativity. It was\nfor showing light\ncomes in particles.\n\nShine light on\ncertain metals and\nelectrons fly out:\nthe photoelectric\neffect. The puzzle\nwas that dim blue\nlight knocked\nelectrons out, while\nbright red light,\nwith far more energy\noverall, knocked out\nnone.\nIn 1905, Einstein\nexplained it: light\ncomes in packets,\nnow called photons,\nwhose energy depends\nonly on their color.\nA blue photon has\nenough energy to\nfree an electron by\nitself. A red one\ndoesn't, and a\nmillion red photons\ncan't team up to do\nit.\n\nThat idea helped\nstart quantum\nphysics and won the\n1921 Nobel Prize.\nYour phone's camera\nworks on the same\nprinciple: each\npixel counts the\nelectrons freed by\nthe photons that hit\nit.\x00"""
    "inflation\x00"""
    "Galaxies may have\ngrown from quantum\njitters, stretched\nto cosmic size in an\ninstant.\n\nThe cosmic microwave\nbackground is almost\nexactly the same\ntemperature\neverywhere, even in\nparts of the sky\nthat should never\nhave been in\ncontact. And space\nis very nearly flat.\nBoth are hard to\nexplain with a plain\nBig Bang.\n\nInflation explains\nthem: in a tiny\nfraction of the\nfirst second, space\nexpanded faster than\nit ever has since,\ndoubling in size at\nleast eighty times\nin a row. Any curve\nwas ironed flat, and\neverything we see\ncame from a patch\nthat had once been\nin contact.\n\nQuantum\nfluctuations, the\ntiny jitters every\nfield has, were\nstretched to\nastronomical size.\nSlightly denser\npatches pulled in\nmatter and grew into\ngalaxies. If so, the\nlargest structures\nin the universe\nbegan as quantum\nuncertainty.\nInflation is widely\naccepted, but not\nyet proven.\x00"""
    "kinds of ice\x00"""
    "There are about\ntwenty kinds of ice.\nSome stay solid at\nhundreds of degrees.\n\nThe ice in your\nfreezer is ice Ih,\nwhere water\nmolecules form a\nhexagonal lattice\nwith plenty of empty\nspace. That is why\nice is less dense\nthan water and\nfloats, which is\nunusual: most solids\nsink in their own\nliquid.\nSqueeze water hard\nenough and its\nmolecules pack into\nother arrangements.\nAbout twenty have\nbeen found, named\nwith Roman numerals.\nIce VII forms at\ntens of thousands of\ntimes air pressure,\nand at higher\npressures can stay\nsolid at hundreds of\ndegrees.\n\nSuch ices probably\nexist deep inside\nicy moons like\nGanymede. One, ice\nXVIII, made with\nlasers in 2019, is\nsuperionic: half\nsolid, half liquid.\nIts oxygen atoms are\nlocked in a crystal\nwhile its hydrogen\nflows through it.\x00"""
    "giant atoms\x00"""
    "An atom can be\npuffed up until it\nis wider than a\nhuman hair.\n\nGive an atom's outer\nelectron more and\nmore energy, without\nquite setting it\nfree, and it moves\nto orbits farther\nand farther out. The\natom's width grows\nwith the square of\nthe energy level's\nnumber.\n\nThese are Rydberg\natoms. With the\nelectron at level\n100, an atom is ten\nthousand times wider\nthan normal, about\nthe size of a\nbacterium. In the\nthin gas between\nstars, astronomers\nhave detected atoms\nat levels near\n1,000: about a tenth\nof a millimetre\nacross.\n\nSuch atoms are\nfragile, but they\nreact strongly to\nelectric fields and\nto each other, even\nfar apart. That\nmakes them useful as\nqubits in some\nquantum computers,\nand as sensors that\ncan pick up radio\nsignals.\x00"""
    "bell's theorem\x00"""
    "Experiments show\nnature can't be\nexplained by\nparticles carrying\nhidden instructions.\n\nMaybe entangled\nparticles are like a\npair of gloves in\ntwo boxes: open one,\nfind a left glove,\nand of course the\nother is right.\nNothing spooky, the\nanswer was there\nfrom the start.\nEinstein hoped\nquantum physics\nworked like that,\nwith 'hidden\nvariables' we just\ncan't see.\n\nIn 1964, John Bell\nfound a way to test\nit. If the answers\nare carried along\nfrom the start, then\nthe results of\nmeasuring pairs at\ndifferent angles can\nonly agree so often.\nQuantum physics\npredicts that they\nagree more often\nthan that.\nExperiments by John\nClauser, Alain\nAspect, Anton\nZeilinger and others\nfound the quantum\nprediction again and\nagain, and in 2015\nwith the last big\nloopholes closed.\nThey won the 2022\nNobel Prize. Nature\nis not like gloves\nin boxes.\x00"""
    "the neutron puzzle\x00"""
    "A lone neutron falls\napart in about 15\nminutes, but two\nways of timing it\ndisagree.\n\nInside a nucleus, a\nneutron can last\nforever. Alone, it\ndecays in under a\nquarter of an hour\ninto a proton, an\nelectron and an\nantineutrino. How\nlong it lasts\nmatters, for example\nto work out how much\nhelium the Big Bang\nmade.\n\nOne way to time it\nis to trap very cold\nneutrons in a\n'bottle' and count\nhow many are left\nafter a while: about\n878 seconds. Another\nis to send a beam of\nneutrons through a\ntrap and count the\nprotons they make:\nabout 888 seconds.\n\nTen seconds sounds\nsmall, but both\nmethods are precise\nenough that the gap\nshouldn't exist.\nEither one of them\nhas an error nobody\nhas found, or some\nneutrons decay in a\nway we don't know\nabout, perhaps into\nsomething invisible.\x00"""
    "neutrino flavors\x00"""
    "Neutrinos change\nidentity as they\nfly, which proved\nthat they have mass.\n\nNeutrinos come in\nthree kinds, or\nflavors: electron,\nmuon and tau. For\ndecades, detectors\ncaught only about a\nthird of the\nelectron neutrinos\nexpected from the\nSun. Either the Sun\nwas misunderstood,\nor the neutrinos\nwere going\nsomewhere.\n\nThey were changing.\nA neutrino of one\nflavor is really a\nquantum blend of\nthree states with\nslightly different\nmasses. As it\ntravels, those\nstates drift out of\nstep and the blend\nshifts, so an\nelectron neutrino\ncan arrive as a muon\nor tau neutrino, and\nlater turn back.\n\nThat is only\npossible if\nneutrinos have mass,\nwhich the standard\ntheory of particles\nsaid they didn't.\nThe discovery won\nthe 2015 Nobel\nPrize. We still\ndon't know their\nexact masses, only\nthat they are more\nthan a million times\nlighter than an\nelectron.\x00"""
    "antimatter falls\x00"""
    "In 2023, antimatter\natoms were dropped\nfor the first time.\nThey fell down, like\nmatter.\n\nAntimatter has the\nopposite charge of\nmatter, so you might\nwonder if it has\nopposite gravity\ntoo, and falls up.\nAlmost every\nphysicist expected\nit to fall down, but\nnobody had checked:\nantimatter is hard\nto make, and gravity\nis so weak that the\nslightest electric\nfield would swamp\nit.\n\nAt CERN, the ALPHA\nexperiment made\nantihydrogen, a\npositron around an\nantiproton, which\nhas no overall\ncharge. It held the\natoms with magnets\nin a tall tube,\nslowly let them go,\nand counted how many\nescaped through the\ntop and the bottom.\nMost came out of the\nbottom: antimatter\nfalls down, at close\nto the same rate as\nmatter. More precise\ntests are on the\nway, looking for\neven a slight\ndifference.\x00"""
    "why matter won\x00"""
    "The Big Bang should\nhave made as much\nantimatter as\nmatter. Why did\nmatter win?\n\nEnergy turns into\nmatter in pairs: one\nparticle and one\nantiparticle. So the\nhot early universe\nshould have made the\nsame amount of each,\nand as it cooled,\nthey should have\ndestroyed each other\ncompletely, leaving\nnothing but light.\nInstead, for every\nbillion or so pairs,\nthere was one extra\nparticle of matter.\nEverything in the\nuniverse today,\nstars, planets and\nyou, is made of\nthose leftovers.\n\nPhysicists have\nmeasured small\ndifferences in how\nmatter and\nantimatter behave,\nin particles called\nkaons and B mesons,\nbut they are far too\nsmall to explain the\nleftovers. Finding\nthe real cause is\none of the biggest\nopen questions in\nphysics.\x00"""
    "the arrow of time\x00"""
    "The laws of physics\nwork the same\nbackwards in time.\nOnly entropy tells\npast from future.\n\nFilm two billiard\nballs colliding and\nplay it backwards:\nit still looks like\na possible\ncollision. Almost\nall the basic laws\nof physics work\nequally well\nforwards and\nbackwards in time.\n\nYet eggs break and\nnever unbreak, and\nmilk mixes into\ncoffee but never\nunmixes. The\ndifference is\nentropy: roughly, a\ncount of how many\nways the parts of\nsomething can be\narranged and still\nlook the same. There\nare vastly more\nmixed arrangements\nthan unmixed ones,\nso things drift\ntoward mixed, just\nby chance.\nSo the past had\nlower entropy than\nthe present. Tracing\nit back, the\nuniverse must have\nbegun in an\nextraordinarily\nlow-entropy state at\nthe Big Bang. Why it\ndid is unknown, and\nit may be the reason\nwe remember the past\nand not the future.\x00"""
    "plasma\x00"""
    "Almost all the\nmatter you can see\nin the universe is\nneither solid,\nliquid nor gas.\n\nHeat a gas enough\nand its atoms lose\nelectrons, leaving a\nsoup of charged\nparticles: plasma,\nthe fourth state of\nmatter. Because its\nparticles are\ncharged, plasma\nconducts electricity\nand is steered by\nmagnetic fields,\nunlike a gas.\n\nStars are made of\nplasma, so over 99\npercent of the\nvisible matter in\nthe universe is in\nthis state. On Earth\nyou see it in\nlightning, neon\nsigns, the aurora\nand the flame of a\nwelding torch.\n\nFusion reactors try\nto hold plasma at\nover 100 million\ndegrees, hotter than\nthe Sun's core, with\nmagnetic fields,\nsince no wall could\ntouch it. Keeping it\nsteady is hard: it\ntwists and wriggles\nalmost like a living\nthing.\x00"""
    "weak gravity\x00"""
    "Gravity is so weak\nthat a little fridge\nmagnet beats the\npull of the whole\nEarth.\n\nGravity holds\nplanets in orbit and\ngalaxies together,\nso it seems\npowerful. But that\nis only because it\nadds up: every bit\nof mass pulls, and\nnothing ever cancels\nit.\n\nCompare it to the\nothers. The electric\npush between two\nelectrons is about\n10^42 times stronger\nthan their\ngravitational pull:\na one followed by 42\nzeros. A tiny magnet\nholds a paper clip\nup against the\nentire planet\npulling it down.\nWhy gravity is so\nmuch weaker than the\nother forces is a\ndeep puzzle, tied to\nwhat physicists call\nthe hierarchy\nproblem. Some ideas\nsay gravity leaks\ninto extra\ndimensions we can't\nsee. So far, there\nis no evidence for\nthem, and nobody\nknows.\x00"""
    "faster than light\x00"""
    "The universe is 13.8\nbillion years old,\nyet we see things 46\nbillion light-years\naway.\n\nNothing can move\nthrough space faster\nthan light. But\nspace itself can\nstretch, and it has\nbeen expanding since\nthe Big Bang,\ncarrying galaxies\napart.\n\nThe light from the\nfarthest things we\ncan see has\ntravelled for about\n13.8 billion years.\nAll that time, the\nspace behind it kept\nstretching, so the\nplace it left is now\nabout 46 billion\nlight-years away.\nGalaxies far enough\naway are moving away\nfrom us faster than\nlight today, and\nthat breaks no law.\n\nBecause the\nexpansion is\nspeeding up, light\nthat some galaxies\nsend out today will\nnever reach us. Over\nhundreds of billions\nof years, every\ngalaxy beyond our\nlocal group will\nfade from view.\x00"""
    "half-lives\x00"""
    "Each radioactive\natom decays at a\nrandom moment, yet a\nlump of them is a\nprecise clock.\n\nA radioactive\nnucleus has no\nmemory and no age. A\ncarbon-14 atom that\nhas existed for\n10,000 years is\nexactly as likely to\ndecay in the next\nsecond as one made\nyesterday. When it\ngoes is truly\nrandom, as far as\nphysics knows.\n\nBut with trillions\nof atoms, the\nrandomness averages\nout: in 5,730 years\nhalf of them decay,\nin the next 5,730\nyears half of the\nrest, and so on.\nThat half-life\nbarely changes,\nwhatever the heat,\npressure or\nchemistry.\n\nLiving things take\nin carbon-14, made\nin the air by cosmic\nrays, and stop when\nthey die. Measuring\nhow much is left in\na bone or a piece of\nwood tells how long\nago it died, back to\nabout 50,000 years.\nUranium, with\nhalf-lives of\nbillions of years,\ndates the Earth\nitself: 4.5 billion\nyears.\x00"""
    "optical tweezers\x00"""
    "Lasers can hold and\nmove single living\ncells, using the\npush and pull of\nlight.\n\nWhen light passes\nthrough a\ntransparent bead, it\nis bent, and bending\nlight changes its\nmomentum. By\nNewton's third law,\nthe bead feels a\npush the other way.\n\nFocus a laser beam\ntightly, and the\nlight bending\nthrough a nearby\nbead always ends up\npulling it toward\nthe brightest point,\nthe focus. The bead\nis caught in optical\ntweezers, and moving\nthe beam moves the\nbead.\n\nArthur Ashkin\ninvented them, and\nwon the 2018 Nobel\nPrize at the age of\n96. Biologists use\nthem to hold\nbacteria without\nharming them,\nstretch single DNA\nmolecules, and\nmeasure the steps of\ntiny molecular\nmotors, a few\nnanometres at a\ntime.\x00"""
    "quantum computers\x00"""
    "A quantum computer\ndoesn't try every\nanswer at once. It\nmakes the wrong\nanswers cancel out.\n\nA bit is 0 or 1. A\nqubit can be in a\nsuperposition of\nboth, and a few\nhundred qubits\ntogether can be in a\nblend of more\ncombinations than\nthere are atoms in\nthe universe.\n\nIt's tempting to say\nthat a quantum\ncomputer tries them\nall in parallel. But\nreading it out gives\nyou just one random\ncombination, and the\nrest are lost.\nTrying everything is\nuseless if you can\nonly see one result.\n\nThe real trick is\ninterference. A\nquantum algorithm is\nchoreographed so\nthat the paths\nleading to wrong\nanswers cancel out,\nlike waves meeting\ncrest to trough,\nwhile the paths to\nthe right answer add\nup. That only works\nfor some problems,\nlike factoring huge\nnumbers or\nsimulating\nmolecules, so\nquantum computers\nwon't replace\nordinary ones.\x00"""
    "magnetism is motion\x00"""
    "Magnetism is\nelectricity seen by\nsomeone moving.\nRelativity turns one\ninto the other.\n\nA wire carrying a\ncurrent has moving\nelectrons and still\npositive charges,\nequal in number, so\noverall it is\nneutral: it doesn't\npull on a still\ncharge outside it.\nYet a charge moving\nalongside the wire\nfeels a force. We\ncall that force\nmagnetic.\n\nNow look from the\nmoving charge's\npoint of view,\nkeeping pace with\nthe electrons.\nRelativity says that\nmoving things are\nshortened, so the\nspacing of the\nelectrons and of the\npositive charges\nchanges differently,\nand the wire is no\nlonger neutral. The\ncharge just feels an\nelectric force.\n\nSo the magnetic\nforce is the\nelectric force, seen\nwhile moving. It is\na relativity effect,\nand the electrons in\na wire drift at less\nthan a millimetre\nper second, but\nthere are so many of\nthem that it adds up\nto forces strong\nenough to drive\nmotors.\x00"""
    "glass\x00"""
    "Glass is not a slow\nliquid. Old windows\nare thick at the\nbottom for another\nreason.\n\nA popular story says\nglass is a liquid\nthat flows very\nslowly, which is why\nold church windows\nare thicker at the\nbottom. It isn't\ntrue.\n\nGlass is an\namorphous solid: its\natoms are frozen in\nplace in a jumbled\narrangement, like a\nsnapshot of a\nliquid, instead of\nthe neat rows of a\ncrystal.\nCalculations show\nthat at room\ntemperature,\nmedieval glass would\nneed far longer than\nthe age of the\nuniverse to sag\nvisibly.\n\nThe real reason is\nhow the glass was\nmade: blown and spun\ninto sheets of\nuneven thickness,\nwhich glaziers often\nfitted with the\nthick edge at the\nbottom. But not\nalways, and some old\nwindows are thicker\nat the top. Exactly\nhow a liquid turns\ninto a glass is\nstill one of the big\nopen questions about\nsolids.\x00"""
    "electron drift\x00"""
    "When you switch on a\nlight, the electrons\nin the wire crawl\nslower than a snail.\n\nFlip a switch and\nthe lamp lights\nalmost instantly, so\nyou might think\nelectrons race\nthrough the wire.\nThey don't. In a\nhousehold wire, they\ndrift at less than a\nmillimetre per\nsecond, and with\nalternating current,\nthey just jiggle\nback and forth.\n\nWhat travels fast is\nthe push: the\nelectromagnetic\nfield, which spreads\nalong the wire at a\nlarge fraction of\nthe speed of light.\nThe wire is already\nfull of electrons,\nand the field nudges\nall of them almost\nat once, like water\nin a full hose that\nflows out the moment\nyou open the tap.\n\nIn fact, the energy\ndoesn't travel\ninside the wire at\nall, but in the\nelectric and\nmagnetic fields in\nthe space around it,\nwhich the wire\nguides.\x00"""
    "oh-my-god particle\x00"""
    "In 1991, one\nparticle from space\nhit the air with the\nenergy of a thrown\nbaseball.\n\nCosmic rays are\nparticles, mostly\nprotons, that rain\non the Earth from\nspace at nearly the\nspeed of light. When\none hits the top of\nthe atmosphere, it\nshatters into a\nshower of billions\nof particles spread\nover kilometres, and\ndetectors on the\nground catch the\nshower.\n\nOn 15 October 1991,\nthe Fly's Eye\ndetector in Utah saw\na shower from a\nparticle with about\n3 x 10^20\nelectronvolts:\naround 50 joules,\nthe energy of a\nbaseball at about 90\nkilometres an hour,\nin one subatomic\nparticle. It was\nnicknamed the\nOh-My-God particle.\n\nThat is tens of\nmillions of times\nmore than the LHC\ngives a proton.\nNobody knows what in\nthe universe can\naccelerate particles\nthat much. In 2021,\nanother one, named\nAmaterasu, came from\na direction where\nthere seems to be\nnothing at all.\x00"""
    "static electricity\x00"""
    "Rub a balloon on\nyour hair and it\nsticks. Physicists\nstill can't fully\nexplain why.\n\nWhen two materials\ntouch and separate,\ncharge moves from\none to the other.\nThis triboelectric\neffect has been\nknown since the\nancient Greeks\nrubbed amber, whose\nGreek name,\nelektron, gave us\nthe word\nelectricity.\n\nBut what actually\nmoves is\nsurprisingly\nunclear. Sometimes\nit is electrons,\nsometimes charged\nions, sometimes tiny\nbits of the material\ntorn off. Even two\npieces of the same\nmaterial can charge\neach other, which\nsimple theories say\nthey shouldn't.\n\nIt matters: static\nsparks can ignite\ndust in factories,\nand charged grains\nof sand or ash make\nlightning in dust\nstorms and volcanic\nplumes. The basic\nphysics of something\nevery child has\ntried is still being\nworked out.\x00"""
    "absolute zero\x00"""
    "You can get closer\nand closer to\nabsolute zero, but\nyou can never reach\nit.\n\nAbsolute zero, minus\n273.15 degrees, is\nthe temperature at\nwhich a system is in\nits lowest possible\nenergy state. Even\ndeep space is about\n2.7 degrees above\nit, warmed by the\nglow of the Big\nBang.\n\nLabs go much colder:\nclouds of atoms have\nbeen cooled to less\nthan a billionth of\na degree above zero.\nBut the third law of\nthermodynamics says\nthat reaching zero\nexactly would take\ninfinitely many\nsteps, because each\ncooling step removes\na smaller share of\nwhat is left.\n\nIn 2023, physicists\nproved a precise\nquantum version:\nreaching absolute\nzero would take\ninfinite energy,\ninfinite time, or an\ninfinitely complex\nmachine. Like the\nspeed of light, it\nis a limit you can\napproach but never\ntouch.\x00"""
    "oklo\x00"""
    "Two billion years\nago, a natural\nnuclear reactor ran\nby itself in what is\nnow Gabon.\n\nIn 1972, workers at\na French plant\nprocessing uranium\nore from Oklo, in\nGabon, noticed that\nsome of it held\nslightly too little\nuranium-235, the\nkind that splits in\nreactors. It was as\nif part of it had\nalready been used.\nIt had. Two billion\nyears ago, natural\nuranium contained\nmuch more\nuranium-235 than\ntoday, about as much\nas reactor fuel.\nWhere groundwater\nseeped into rich\ndeposits, it slowed\ndown neutrons enough\nto start a chain\nreaction.\n\nThe reactors ran on\nand off for hundreds\nof thousands of\nyears, in cycles:\nthe heat boiled the\nwater away, which\nstopped the\nreaction, until\nwater seeped back.\nTheir leftovers have\nbeen used to check\nthat constants of\nnature, like the\nfine-structure\nconstant, haven't\nchanged since.\x00"""
    "hawking radiation\x00"""
    "Black holes are not\ncompletely black.\nThey glow faintly\nand slowly\nevaporate.\n\nIn 1974, Stephen\nHawking combined\nquantum physics with\ngeneral relativity\nat the edge of a\nblack hole, and\nfound something\nnobody expected: a\nblack hole should\ngive off a faint\nglow of particles,\nas if it had a\ntemperature.\n\nThe bigger the black\nhole, the colder the\nglow. For one the\nmass of the Sun, it\nis about 60\nbillionths of a\ndegree above\nabsolute zero: far\ncolder than space,\nso it takes in more\nthan it gives off.\nHawking radiation\nhas never been\ndetected.\n\nBut in the far\nfuture, as the\nuniverse cools,\nblack holes will\nslowly lose mass\nthis way and\nevaporate, ending in\na final flash. For\none like the Sun,\nthat takes about\n10^67 years. What\nhappens to\neverything that fell\nin is one of the\ndeepest puzzles in\nphysics: the\ninformation paradox.\x00"""
    "superfluids\x00"""
    "Superfluid helium\nflows with no\nfriction. It can\ncreep up the walls\nand out of its cup.\n\nBelow about minus\n271 degrees Celsius,\nliquid helium turns\ninto a superfluid.\nPart of it acts as a\nsingle quantum\nstate, a bit like a\nBose-Einstein\ncondensate, and a\nsingle state has\nnothing to rub\nagainst: it flows\nwith no viscosity at\nall.\n\nSo it leaks through\ncracks too fine for\nany gas. Put it in a\ncup, and a film\nabout a hundred\natoms thick creeps\nup the inside wall,\nover the rim and\ndown the outside,\nuntil the cup is\nempty.\n\nSpin a bucket of it\nand the superfluid\ndoesn't simply turn\nalong. It fills with\ntiny whirlpools,\neach carrying\nexactly the same\namount of swirl,\nbecause in a quantum\nfluid even swirling\ncomes in fixed\nsteps.\x00"""
    "the big bang's light\x00"""
    "Some of the static\non an old TV was\nlight left over from\nthe Big Bang.\n\nAbout 380,000 years\nafter the Big Bang,\nthe universe cooled\nenough for atoms to\nform, and became\ntransparent. The\nlight set free then\nhas been travelling\never since.\n\nThe expansion of the\nuniverse has\nstretched it from\nthe glow of a hot\ngas into microwaves,\nat just 2.7 degrees\nabove absolute zero.\nIt comes from every\ndirection in the\nsky: the cosmic\nmicrowave\nbackground.\n\nTwo radio engineers\nfound it by accident\nin 1964, and first\nblamed pigeon\ndroppings in their\nantenna. About one\npercent of the snowy\nstatic on an old TV\nbetween channels was\nthis ancient light.\nTiny ripples in it,\none part in 100,000,\nwere the seeds of\ngalaxies.\x00"""
    "magnets are quantum\x00"""
    "Classical physics\nproves magnets can't\nexist. Every fridge\nmagnet is a quantum\nobject.\n\nIn 1911 and 1919,\nNiels Bohr and\nHendrika van Leeuwen\nproved a surprising\ntheorem: in\nclassical physics, a\nmaterial in balance\nat any temperature\ncan never be\nmagnetic. The\neffects of its\nmoving electrons\nalways cancel\nexactly.\n\nYet magnets exist,\nso classical physics\nmust be missing\nsomething. Magnetism\ncomes from the\nelectron's spin, a\npurely quantum\nproperty, together\nwith the Pauli\nexclusion principle.\n\nIn iron, the\nexclusion principle\nmakes it cost less\nenergy for\nneighbouring\nelectrons to point\ntheir spins the same\nway. Billions line\nup, and their tiny\nfields add up to one\nyou can feel. Heat\niron past its Curie\ntemperature, 770\ndegrees, and the\nspins scramble: the\nmagnetism\ndisappears.\x00"""
    "the lazy sun\x00"""
    "Your body makes more\nheat per litre than\nthe core of the Sun.\n\nThe Sun's core is\nabout 15 million\ndegrees and\nenormous, which is\nwhy the Sun shines\nso brightly. But for\nits volume, its\nfusion is\nsurprisingly slow:\nabout 300 watts per\ncubic metre at the\nvery centre, like a\ncompost heap.\n\nA resting human\nmakes about 100\nwatts in a body of\nabout 0.07 cubic\nmetres: over 1,000\nwatts per cubic\nmetre, several times\nmore.\n\nThe Sun is bright\nbecause its core is\nhuge, not because it\nis intense. That\nslowness lets it\nburn steadily for 10\nbillion years. It is\nalso why fusion\nreactors on Earth\nmust run much hotter\nthan the Sun's core:\nthey can't wait\nbillions of years\nfor protons to fuse.\x00"""
    "the leidenfrost drop\x00"""
    "Water lasts longer\non a very hot pan\nthan on a warm one:\nit floats on its own\nsteam.\n\nFlick drops of water\non a pan at 100\ndegrees and they\nsizzle away at once.\nOn a pan above about\n200 degrees, they\nskitter around for a\nminute or more.\n\nAt that temperature,\nthe bottom of the\ndrop boils so fast\nthat it makes a thin\ncushion of steam,\nand the drop floats\non it. Steam carries\nheat poorly, so the\ndrop is insulated\nfrom the pan and\nevaporates slowly.\n\nThat is the\nLeidenfrost effect,\ndescribed by a\ndoctor in 1756. It\nis why liquid\nnitrogen spilled on\na floor races around\nin beads, and why a\nwet finger can be\ndipped very briefly\ninto molten lead\nwithout harm (please\ndon't).\x00"""
    "mirages\x00"""
    "A mirage is not a\ntrick of the mind.\nIt's a real image\nthat you can\nphotograph.\n\nLight travels\nslightly faster\nthrough hot, thin\nair than through\ncool, dense air. So\nwhen it passes\nthrough layers of\ndifferent\ntemperatures, it\nbends, curving\ntoward the cooler\nair.\nOver a hot road, the\nair right at the\nsurface is much\nhotter than the air\nabove. Light from\nthe sky heading down\ntoward the road\ncurves back up into\nyour eyes, so you\nsee a patch of sky\non the road ahead,\nwhich looks like\nwater. A camera sees\nit too.\n\nOver cold water, the\nopposite happens:\nships beyond the\nhorizon can appear\nin the sky,\nstretched into\ntowers. This kind of\nmirage, the Fata\nMorgana, may explain\nold tales of ghost\nships.\x00"""
    "the planck length\x00"""
    "Below a length\ncalled the Planck\nlength, our ideas of\nspace and time stop\nworking.\n\nCombine three\nconstants of nature,\nthe speed of light,\nthe strength of\ngravity and Planck's\nconstant from\nquantum physics, and\nyou get a length:\nabout 1.6 x 10^-35\nmetres, a hundred\nbillion billion\ntimes smaller than a\nproton.\n\nAt that size, the\nuncertainty\nprinciple says the\nenergy packed into a\nregion would be so\ngreat that it would\nmake a tiny black\nhole. So measuring\nanything smaller\nseems impossible,\nand there general\nrelativity, which\ntreats space as\nsmooth, clashes\ncompletely with\nquantum physics.\nIt's not proven that\nspace is made of\nlittle pixels of\nthis size. It is the\nscale where we need\na theory of quantum\ngravity, such as\nstring theory or\nloop quantum\ngravity, and no\nexperiment can reach\nit yet.\x00"""
    "oobleck\x00"""
    "Cornflour and water\nmake a liquid you\ncan run across, but\nyou sink if you\nstand still.\n\nMost liquids, like\nwater, flow the same\nway however fast you\npush them. Isaac\nNewton described\ntheir behaviour, so\nthey are called\nNewtonian.\n\nA thick mix of\ncornflour and water\nis not. Stir it\nslowly and it flows.\nHit it and it acts\nlike a solid. The\ngrains are packed so\nclosely that when\npushed fast, they\njam against each\nother and lock into\nchains that bear the\nforce.\n\nPeople have run\nacross pools of it,\nand sunk as soon as\nthey stopped.\nEngineers use the\nsame effect in\nprotective gear that\nstays soft until it\nis struck.\x00"""
    "metamaterials\x00"""
    "Materials built from\ntiny patterns can\nbend light\nbackwards, which no\nnatural one does.\n\nHow a material\nhandles light\nusually depends on\nits atoms.\nMetamaterials depend\non structure\ninstead: repeating\npatterns, like tiny\nmetal rings and\nwires, smaller than\nthe wavelength of\nthe light they are\nmade for.\n\nWith the right\npattern, a\nmetamaterial can\nhave a negative\nrefractive index:\nlight entering it\nbends the wrong way,\nto the same side it\ncame from. A straw\nin a glass of such\nstuff would seem to\nbend back out of the\nliquid.\n\nIn 2006, a\nmetamaterial cloak\nmade a copper\ncylinder nearly\ninvisible to\nmicrowaves, by\nguiding them around\nit like water around\na rock. Doing the\nsame for visible\nlight, in all\ncolors, remains very\nhard.\x00"""
    "the critical point\x00"""
    "Heat water under\nenough pressure and\nits liquid and its\ngas become the same\nthing.\n\nNormally, a liquid\nand its vapor are\nclearly different:\none dense, the other\nthin, with a surface\nbetween them. Heat\nwater in a sealed\ncontainer and the\nliquid expands while\nmore and more vapor\nis squeezed in above\nit, so they grow\ncloser in density.\n\nAt 374 degrees and\n218 times air\npressure they become\nidentical, and the\nsurface between them\ndisappears. Beyond\nthis critical point\nthere is only a\nsupercritical fluid,\nwhich flows through\nthings like a gas\nand dissolves things\nlike a liquid.\n\nSupercritical carbon\ndioxide is used to\ntake the caffeine\nout of coffee beans.\nAnd right at the\ncritical point,\nfluids turn cloudy\nand shimmer, because\ntheir density\nflickers at every\nscale at once.\x00"""
    "aurora colors\x00"""
    "The red of an aurora\ncomes from oxygen\natoms that wait\nnearly two minutes\nto glow.\n\nAuroras form when\nparticles from the\nSun, guided toward\nthe poles by Earth's\nmagnetic field,\ncrash into the upper\natmosphere and give\ntheir energy to its\natoms.\n\nOxygen atoms give\nthat energy off as\ngreen or red light.\nThese are\n'forbidden'\ntransitions: so\nunlikely that the\natom waits, almost a\nsecond for green and\nnearly two minutes\nfor red, before it\nglows. In the denser\nair lower down, it\nwould bump into\nanother molecule\nfirst and lose its\nenergy without any\nlight.\n\nThat is why the red\nonly glows very high\nup, above about 200\nkilometres, where\nthe air is thin\nenough to wait\nundisturbed, and the\ngreen a bit lower.\nBlue and purple come\nfrom nitrogen. For\ndecades the green\npuzzled scientists,\nbecause no lab could\nmake it.\x00"""
    "lasers\x00"""
    "A laser works\nbecause light can\nmake an atom give\noff a perfect copy\nof that same light.\n\nAn atom with extra\nenergy can drop to a\nlower energy and\ngive off a photon, a\nparticle of light,\non its own. But in\n1917 Einstein\nrealized that a\npassing photon can\nalso trigger the\ndrop. The new photon\nis then an exact\ntwin of the one that\ntriggered it: same\ncolor, same\ndirection, in step.\n\nThat is stimulated\nemission. In a\nlaser, most of the\natoms are pumped up\nto the higher\nenergy, and mirrors\nbounce the light\nback and forth\nthrough them. Each\nphoton makes twins,\nwhich make more\ntwins, and an\navalanche of\nidentical light\nbuilds up.\n\nLaser stands for\nlight amplification\nby stimulated\nemission of\nradiation. Because\nits light is all in\nstep, it can be\nfocused to a tiny\nspot, read the bumps\non a disc, and\ntravel to the Moon\nand back.\x00"""
    "quantum hall effect\x00"""
    "Cold electrons in a\nmagnet show a\nresistance set only\nby nature's\nconstants.\n\nElectrons moving\nthrough a flat sheet\nin a strong magnetic\nfield are pushed\nsideways, which\nbuilds up a voltage\nacross the sheet.\nThat is the Hall\neffect, known since\n1879.\n\nIn 1980, Klaus von\nKlitzing cooled such\na sheet close to\nabsolute zero and\nfound that its\nsideways resistance\ndid not change\nsmoothly with the\nfield. It stood on\nflat steps, at a\nnatural value\ndivided by exactly\n1, 2, 3 and so on,\nthe same in every\nsample, whatever its\nsize or impurities.\n\nThat value is\nPlanck's constant\ndivided by the\nsquare of the\nelectron's charge:\nabout 25,813 ohms.\nIt is now how the\nworld defines the\nohm. It is so exact\nbecause the\nelectrons' paths\ndepend on the\noverall shape of\ntheir quantum\nstates, a property\ncalled topology.\x00"""
    "graphene\x00"""
    "Graphene is carbon\none atom thick, and\none of the strongest\nmaterials ever\ntested.\n\nPencil lead is\ngraphite: stacks of\nflat sheets of\ncarbon atoms in\nhexagons, like\nchicken wire. In\n2004, Andre Geim and\nKonstantin Novoselov\npeeled off single\nsheets with sticky\ntape, which won them\nthe 2010 Nobel\nPrize.\n\nA single layer,\ngraphene, is about\n200 times stronger\nthan steel of the\nsame thickness, and\nit carries heat and\nelectricity\nextremely well.\n\nIts electrons are\nstrange: they move\nas if they had no\nmass, obeying the\nsame kind of\nequation as\nparticles near light\nspeed, only 300\ntimes slower. And\ntwo sheets stacked\nwith a twist of\nabout 1.1 degrees\nbecome a\nsuperconductor.\x00"""
    "structural color\x00"""
    "A morpho butterfly's\nblue contains no\nblue pigment. It is\nmade of shapes, not\ndyes.\n\nMost colors come\nfrom pigments,\nmolecules that\nabsorb some colors\nof light and reflect\nthe rest. A morpho\nbutterfly's wings\nare covered in\nscales with tiny\nridges, layered like\nlittle Christmas\ntrees, spaced about\nas far apart as a\nwavelength of light.\n\nLight reflected from\neach layer\ninterferes with the\nlight from the\nothers. For blue,\nthe reflections line\nup and add up; for\nother colors, they\ncancel. The same\nthing makes the\ncolors of soap\nbubbles and peacock\nfeathers.\n\nBecause it is made\nof shape, not\nchemicals, a\nstructural color\ndoesn't fade. Soak\nthe wing in a liquid\nthat fills the gaps,\nand the blue changes\nor vanishes, because\nthe spacing that\nmade it is gone.\x00""";
